with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$In adults, the primary site of haematopoiesis is the:$x$, $x$Red bone marrow$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Red bone marrow
HAEMATOPOIESIS means the making of blood cells (red cells, white cells and platelets). In ADULTS it happens mainly in the RED BONE MARROW of the flat bones and the ends of long bones — the sternum, ribs, vertebrae, pelvis (iliac crest), skull and the upper ends of the femur and humerus. All blood cells come from one parent cell, the haematopoietic STEM cell, found in this marrow. That is why bone marrow aspiration/biopsy is taken from the iliac crest or sternum when we want to study blood disorders such as leukaemia or aplastic anaemia. In the unborn baby the order is different: yolk sac first, then liver and spleen, then bone marrow by birth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Liver — The LIVER is the main site of blood formation in the FOETUS (middle months of pregnancy), not in the healthy adult. It only returns to this job in disease (extramedullary haematopoiesis, e.g. thalassaemia, myelofibrosis).
• Spleen — The SPLEEN filters and destroys old red cells and stores platelets and lymphocytes; it makes blood cells only in the foetus or in marrow failure.
• Lymph nodes — LYMPH NODES filter lymph and help lymphocytes to multiply and mature, but they do not make red cells or platelets.

⭐ Key point: Adult blood cells are made in RED BONE MARROW (sternum, pelvis, vertebrae, ribs, skull). Foetus: yolk sac → liver/spleen → marrow.$x$, null),
(2, $x$A patient develops severe hypocalcemia with perioral tingling, muscle cramps, and a positive Chvostek sign after total thyroidectomy. Which complication is most likely?$x$, $x$Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands
After TOTAL THYROIDECTOMY the small PARATHYROID GLANDS (usually four, sitting behind the thyroid) can be removed by mistake or lose their blood supply. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) keeps blood calcium up, so without it calcium falls (HYPOCALCAEMIA), usually 24–72 hours after surgery. Low calcium makes nerves and muscles over-excitable: tingling around the mouth and fingers, muscle cramps, CHVOSTEK sign (tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear makes the face twitch), TROUSSEAU sign (BP cuff inflated makes the hand go into spasm), and in severe cases laryngospasm, seizures and arrhythmia (long QT). Nursing: keep IV calcium gluconate 10% at the bedside, check serum calcium, report tingling early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid storm — THYROID STORM gives high fever, fast heart rate, agitation and sweating — a hypermetabolic picture, not low-calcium tetany.
• Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury — RECURRENT LARYNGEAL NERVE injury causes HOARSENESS or a weak voice (and stridor if both sides are cut), not tingling or a positive Chvostek sign.
• Adrenal insufficiency — ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY gives low BP, weakness, low sodium and high potassium; it is not linked to thyroid surgery.

⭐ Key point: Tingling + cramps + Chvostek/Trousseau after thyroidectomy = HYPOCALCAEMIA from parathyroid injury. Give IV calcium gluconate; keep it at the bedside.$x$, null),
(3, $x$A normal adult's total lung capacity is approximately:$x$, $x$6000 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6000 mL
TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY (TLC) is the total air in the lungs after the deepest possible breath in. In a normal adult it is about 6000 mL (6 litres) — a little less in women and smaller people. TLC = vital capacity (about 4600–4800 mL) + residual volume (about 1200 mL, the air that always stays so the alveoli don't collapse). Other volumes to remember: tidal volume about 500 mL (normal quiet breath), inspiratory reserve about 3000 mL, expiratory reserve about 1100–1200 mL. TLC goes DOWN in restrictive disease (pulmonary fibrosis, obesity, chest wall deformity) and goes UP (hyperinflation) in obstructive disease like emphysema/COPD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1500 mL — 1500 mL is too small; it is close to the residual volume plus a bit, not the whole lung.
• 2500 mL — 2500 mL is roughly the functional residual capacity (air left after a normal breath out), not total capacity.
• 10000 mL — 10000 mL (10 L) is far above normal for any adult human lung.

⭐ Key point: TLC ≈ 6000 mL; tidal volume ≈ 500 mL; residual volume ≈ 1200 mL; vital capacity ≈ 4800 mL.$x$, null),
(4, $x$The dermis contains all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Keratinocytes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Keratinocytes
The skin has two main layers. The top layer, the EPIDERMIS, is made mostly of KERATINOCYTES — cells that make keratin, the tough protein that waterproofs and protects the skin. The epidermis has NO blood vessels; it is fed by diffusion from below. Under it is the DERMIS, a thick connective-tissue layer that holds blood vessels, nerve endings (touch, pain, pressure, temperature receptors), hair follicles, sweat glands, sebaceous (oil) glands, collagen and elastin. So the item NOT found as a main dermal component is the keratinocyte — it belongs to the epidermis. Remember: "E for Epidermis, K for Keratin on the outside; D for Dermis, Deep with vessels and nerves."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood vessels — Blood vessels ARE in the dermis; they feed the skin and help control body temperature by dilating or constricting.
• Nerve endings — Nerve endings ARE in the dermis; they let us feel touch, pain, heat and pressure.
• Hair follicles — Hair follicles are rooted in the dermis (and reach into the subcutaneous layer), along with their sebaceous glands.

⭐ Key point: Keratinocytes = EPIDERMIS. Dermis = vessels, nerves, hair follicles, glands, collagen. The epidermis is avascular.$x$, null),
(5, $x$The hypothalamus links the nervous and endocrine systems and regulates all of the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$voluntary skeletal muscle coordination$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: voluntary skeletal muscle coordination
The HYPOTHALAMUS is a small area at the base of the brain that links the nervous system to the endocrine system through the pituitary gland. It is the body's "thermostat and control centre": it regulates body temperature, hunger and satiety, thirst and water balance (makes ADH), the sleep–wake (circadian) rhythm, emotions and stress responses, and controls pituitary hormones. It does NOT coordinate voluntary skeletal muscle movement. Smooth, accurate voluntary movement and balance are the job of the CEREBELLUM, working with the motor cortex and basal ganglia. That is why cerebellar disease causes ataxia, tremor on movement and poor coordination, while hypothalamic damage causes problems like fever, diabetes insipidus or abnormal appetite.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• body temperature — Body temperature IS regulated by the hypothalamus — that is why infection "resets" it and causes fever.
• circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle — Circadian rhythm and sleep–wake cycle ARE controlled by the hypothalamus (suprachiasmatic nucleus).
• hunger and thirst — Hunger and thirst ARE controlled by the hypothalamus (feeding/satiety centres; thirst and ADH).

⭐ Key point: Hypothalamus = temperature, hunger, thirst, sleep, hormones. Coordination of voluntary movement = CEREBELLUM.$x$, null),
(6, $x$Gaseous exchange in the lungs occurs primarily at the level of the:$x$, $x$Alveoli$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
Gas exchange (oxygen in, carbon dioxide out) happens in the ALVEOLI — tiny air sacs at the end of the airways. There are about 300–500 million of them, giving a surface area of about 70 m² (half a tennis court). Their walls are only one cell thick and are wrapped in capillaries, so the gap between air and blood (the respiratory membrane) is extremely thin (about 0.5 micrometres). Oxygen diffuses from alveolus into blood, CO2 from blood into alveolus. Type I cells form the thin wall; Type II cells make surfactant to stop alveoli collapsing. Diseases that thicken the wall (fibrosis), fill the alveoli (pneumonia, pulmonary oedema) or destroy them (emphysema) all reduce gas exchange.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trachea — The TRACHEA is a conducting airway; it warms, humidifies and filters air but no gas exchange happens there (it is part of anatomical dead space).
• Bronchi — The BRONCHI are also conducting airways (dead space); their walls are too thick for gas exchange.
• Larynx — The LARYNX is the voice box and protects the airway; it plays no part in gas exchange.

⭐ Key point: Gas exchange = ALVEOLI (huge area, very thin wall). Trachea, bronchi and larynx only conduct air (dead space).$x$, null),
(7, $x$Which epidermal layer is found only in thick (glabrous) skin, such as the palms and soles?$x$, $x$Stratum lucidum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stratum lucidum
The epidermis has up to five layers (deep to surface): stratum BASALE, SPINOSUM, GRANULOSUM, LUCIDUM and CORNEUM. Memory aid: "Come, Let's Get Sun Burnt" (from top: Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale). The STRATUM LUCIDUM is a thin clear layer of dead, flattened cells containing eleidin. It is found ONLY in THICK (glabrous, hairless) skin — the PALMS of the hands and SOLES of the feet — where extra friction and pressure need extra protection. Thin skin over most of the body has only four layers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratum spinosum — STRATUM SPINOSUM ("prickly layer") is present in both thick and thin skin; it gives strength and contains Langerhans (immune) cells.
• Stratum granulosum — STRATUM GRANULOSUM is present in both; its cells start making keratin and waterproofing lipids and begin to die.
• Stratum basale — STRATUM BASALE is present in all skin; it is the deepest layer, where new cells are made by division and where melanocytes sit.

⭐ Key point: Stratum LUCIDUM = only in thick skin of palms and soles. Order deep→surface: Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum, Corneum.$x$, null),
(8, $x$Most nutrient absorption occurs in the:$x$, $x$Small intestine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Small intestine
Most nutrient absorption takes place in the SMALL INTESTINE (duodenum, jejunum, ileum). It is about 6 metres long and its lining is folded into circular folds, VILLI and MICROVILLI, giving a huge surface (about 200–250 m²) for absorption. Carbohydrates (as glucose), proteins (as amino acids), fats (as fatty acids into lacteals), vitamins and minerals are absorbed here. Special sites: iron and calcium mainly in the duodenum; most nutrients in the jejunum; vitamin B12 and bile salts in the terminal ILEUM. That is why removing the ileum causes B12 deficiency, and diseases like coeliac disease (damaged villi) cause malabsorption.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stomach — The STOMACH mainly stores and mixes food and starts protein digestion with acid and pepsin; it absorbs only a little (water, alcohol, some drugs like aspirin).
• Large intestine — The LARGE INTESTINE mainly absorbs WATER and electrolytes and forms stool; very few nutrients are absorbed there (some vitamins made by gut bacteria, e.g. vitamin K).
• Oesophagus — The OESOPHAGUS simply carries food from the mouth to the stomach by peristalsis; it absorbs nothing.

⭐ Key point: Most absorption = SMALL INTESTINE (villi + microvilli). Iron → duodenum; B12 → terminal ileum; water → large intestine.$x$, null),
(9, $x$A patient with severe burns loses significant fluid through the damaged skin. This highlights the skin's normal physiological role in:$x$, $x$Fluid/temperature regulation and acting as a protective barrier against fluid loss and infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluid/temperature regulation and acting as a protective barrier against fluid loss and infection
Intact skin is a barrier. It stops body water from evaporating away, keeps out germs, and helps control temperature through sweating and changes in blood flow. In SEVERE BURNS this barrier is destroyed, so the patient loses large amounts of fluid, protein and heat through the wound, and bacteria can enter easily. Capillaries also become leaky, so fluid shifts out of the blood. This explains why burns patients need aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula 4 mL × kg × %TBSA in 24 h, half in the first 8 h), warming to prevent hypothermia, and strict infection control. The question shows the skin's normal role in FLUID AND TEMPERATURE REGULATION and as a PROTECTIVE BARRIER.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hormone production only — "Hormone production ONLY" is wrong: the skin does help make vitamin D, but that is not what fluid loss in burns shows, and "only" makes it false.
• Filtering blood — FILTERING BLOOD is the job of the kidneys (and the spleen/liver), not the skin.
• Producing red blood cells — PRODUCING RED BLOOD CELLS is the job of the red bone marrow, not the skin.

⭐ Key point: Skin = barrier against fluid loss, infection and heat loss. Burns remove it → fluid loss, hypothermia, infection.$x$, null),
(10, $x$The kidneys are classified as retroperitoneal organs because they:$x$, $x$Lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity
The PERITONEUM is the thin membrane lining the abdominal cavity. Organs inside it, hung on a mesentery (e.g. stomach, most of the small bowel, transverse colon), are INTRAPERITONEAL. Organs that lie BEHIND the posterior parietal peritoneum, against the back wall of the abdomen, are RETROPERITONEAL. The KIDNEYS are retroperitoneal — covered by peritoneum only on their front surface. Other retroperitoneal organs, memory aid "SAD PUCKER": Suprarenal (adrenal) glands, Aorta/IVC, Duodenum (most), Pancreas (except tail), Ureters, Colon (ascending and descending), Kidneys, Esophagus, Rectum. Clinical link: kidney surgery can be done through the flank without opening the peritoneal cavity, and retroperitoneal bleeding may show as flank bruising (Grey Turner sign).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Are suspended by a mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity — Being SUSPENDED BY A MESENTERY inside the cavity is the definition of an INTRAPERITONEAL organ — the opposite.
• Float freely within the peritoneal cavity — No solid organ "floats freely" in the peritoneal cavity; all are anchored, and the kidneys are fixed in fat and fascia behind the peritoneum.
• Lie entirely anterior to the peritoneum, outside the abdomen — The kidneys lie POSTERIOR (behind) the peritoneum, inside the abdomen — not anterior to it and not outside the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: Retroperitoneal = BEHIND the parietal peritoneum. Kidneys, adrenals, pancreas, aorta, ureters ("SAD PUCKER").$x$, null),
(11, $x$Which meningeal layer lies closest to the brain surface?$x$, $x$Pia mater$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pia mater
The brain and spinal cord are covered by three MENINGES. From outside in, the memory aid is "PAD" read backwards — Dura, Arachnoid, Pia (D-A-P from outside). DURA MATER ("tough mother") is the thick outer layer next to the skull. ARACHNOID MATER is the middle, web-like layer; under it is the SUBARACHNOID space containing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and big blood vessels. PIA MATER ("gentle mother") is the thinnest, innermost layer, stuck firmly to the brain surface and following every fold (gyri and sulci). Clinical: a subarachnoid haemorrhage bleeds into the space above the pia; lumbar puncture collects CSF from the subarachnoid space; meningitis is inflammation of the arachnoid and pia (leptomeninges).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dura mater — DURA MATER is the OUTERMOST meninx, lying against the inside of the skull — the farthest from the brain.
• Arachnoid mater — ARACHNOID MATER is the MIDDLE layer, separated from the brain by the CSF-filled subarachnoid space.
• Periosteum — PERIOSTEUM covers bone (the skull), so it is outside all three meninges; it is not a meningeal layer at all.

⭐ Key point: Meninges outside → in: Dura, Arachnoid, PIA. Pia is the one stuck on the brain surface.$x$, null),
(12, $x$The brainstem consists of:$x$, $x$Midbrain, pons and medulla$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Midbrain, pons and medulla
The BRAINSTEM connects the brain to the spinal cord and has three parts, top to bottom: MIDBRAIN, PONS and MEDULLA OBLONGATA. It contains the nuclei of cranial nerves III–XII, the centres for breathing, heart rate and blood pressure (mainly the medulla), and the reticular activating system that keeps us awake. That is why brainstem damage or herniation (from raised ICP) causes coma, abnormal pupils, irregular breathing and Cushing's triad (high BP, slow pulse, irregular breathing), and why brainstem reflexes are tested for brain death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain — "Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain" are the three main developmental divisions of the WHOLE brain; the forebrain (cerebrum, thalamus) is not part of the brainstem.
• Hindbrain, ventricle and pons — A VENTRICLE is a CSF-filled cavity (the fourth ventricle lies behind the pons and medulla), not a part of the brainstem.
• Midbrain, pons and forebrain — The FOREBRAIN (cerebral hemispheres, thalamus, hypothalamus) sits above the brainstem, so it cannot be part of it.

⭐ Key point: Brainstem = MIDBRAIN + PONS + MEDULLA. Medulla holds vital breathing and cardiovascular centres.$x$, null),
(13, $x$The lymphatic system's primary functions include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Directly pumping oxygenated blood to tissues$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Directly pumping oxygenated blood to tissues
The LYMPHATIC SYSTEM is a network of vessels, nodes and organs (spleen, thymus, tonsils). Its main jobs: (1) DRAIN excess tissue fluid — about 2–3 litres a day leak from capillaries and are returned to the blood via the thoracic duct into the left subclavian vein; (2) ABSORB DIETARY FATS from the gut through lacteals in the villi (as chylomicrons); (3) support IMMUNITY — lymph nodes filter germs and house lymphocytes. The lymphatic system has NO heart pumping it; lymph moves by muscle squeezing, breathing and one-way valves. Pumping oxygenated blood to the tissues is the job of the HEART and arteries (cardiovascular system). Blocked lymph drainage causes lymphoedema (e.g. after axillary node removal in breast surgery, or in filariasis/elephantiasis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Draining excess interstitial fluid back into the bloodstream — Draining excess interstitial fluid back to the blood IS a main lymphatic function; failure causes lymphoedema.
• Transporting absorbed dietary fats from the intestine — Transporting absorbed fats from the intestine IS a lymphatic function (lacteals carry chyle).
• Supporting immune function — Supporting immune function IS a key function (lymph nodes, spleen, lymphocytes).

⭐ Key point: Lymphatics = fluid return, fat absorption, immunity. The HEART pumps oxygenated blood — lymphatics have no pump.$x$, null),
(14, $x$During subclavian central line insertion, the vein lies:$x$, $x$Anterior to the subclavian artery, posterior to the clavicle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anterior to the subclavian artery, posterior to the clavicle
The SUBCLAVIAN VEIN runs under the CLAVICLE (collarbone), over the first rib. It lies IN FRONT OF (anterior to) the subclavian ARTERY, separated from it by the anterior scalene muscle, and BEHIND (posterior to) the clavicle. For a subclavian central line the needle is passed under the clavicle aiming towards the sternal notch. Knowing this anatomy explains the main risks: going too deep can puncture the subclavian ARTERY (bright red pulsatile blood, haematoma that cannot be compressed) or the PLEURA, causing a PNEUMOTHORAX — which is why a chest X-ray is done after insertion to check the tip position (lower SVC/cavo-atrial junction) and exclude pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lateral to the carotid artery only — "Lateral to the carotid artery only" describes the INTERNAL JUGULAR vein in the neck, not the subclavian vein.
• Superior to the clavicle at all times — The subclavian vein is mostly BELOW/behind the clavicle, not above it at all times.
• Within the mediastinum only — It is not "within the mediastinum only"; it is in the root of the neck/upper chest and only joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein near the mediastinum.

⭐ Key point: Subclavian vein = ANTERIOR to the subclavian artery, POSTERIOR to the clavicle. Main risks: arterial puncture and pneumothorax → do a chest X-ray.$x$, null),
(15, $x$The spinal cord's reflex action allows for:$x$, $x$A rapid, automatic response to a stimulus, bypassing the need for the brain to process it first$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid, automatic response to a stimulus, bypassing the need for the brain to process it first
A REFLEX is a fast, automatic, involuntary response to a stimulus. The signal travels along a REFLEX ARC: receptor → sensory (afferent) neurone → spinal cord (often via an interneurone) → motor (efferent) neurone → effector (muscle). The response happens at the SPINAL CORD level, before the brain has processed the sensation — so you pull your hand off a hot object before you feel pain. Examples: knee jerk (patellar reflex, L3–L4), withdrawal reflex, Babinski sign. Nurses test reflexes in neurological assessment: absent reflexes suggest lower motor neurone or spinal shock; brisk reflexes suggest upper motor neurone lesion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conscious decision-making before every movement — Reflexes are NOT conscious decisions; conscious control comes from the cerebral cortex and is slower.
• Only sensory perception with no motor output — A reflex has both SENSORY input and MOTOR output; "only sensory perception with no motor output" is not a reflex.
• Hormone regulation — Hormone regulation is done by the endocrine system and hypothalamus, not by spinal reflex arcs.

⭐ Key point: Spinal reflex = rapid, automatic response through the reflex arc, without waiting for the brain.$x$, null),
(16, $x$Spontaneous bleeding risk significantly increases when the platelet count falls below approximately:$x$, $x$10,000–20,000/microL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10,000–20,000/microL
The normal platelet count is 150,000–400,000/microL (150–400 × 10⁹/L). Below 150,000 is THROMBOCYTOPENIA, but the bleeding risk depends on how low it goes: below about 50,000 there is bleeding with surgery or trauma; below 20,000 there is risk of SPONTANEOUS bleeding (petechiae, gum bleeding, nosebleeds); below 10,000 there is high risk of serious internal bleeding, including into the brain. Prophylactic platelet transfusion is usually given below 10,000 (or below 20,000 with fever/sepsis). Nursing care: bleeding precautions — soft toothbrush, no IM injections, avoid aspirin/NSAIDs, electric razor, prevent falls, check stool/urine for blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150,000/microL — 150,000/microL is the LOWER LIMIT OF NORMAL; spontaneous bleeding does not occur here.
• 100,000/microL — 100,000/microL is mild thrombocytopenia; bleeding is unlikely without trauma or surgery.
• 250,000/microL — 250,000/microL is a completely normal count.

⭐ Key point: Spontaneous bleeding risk rises below 10,000–20,000/microL. Normal platelets 150,000–400,000.$x$, null),
(17, $x$Winter's formula is used to determine the expected respiratory compensation for:$x$, $x$A primary metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A primary metabolic acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA tells you what the PaCO2 SHOULD be when the lungs are properly compensating for a PRIMARY METABOLIC ACIDOSIS: Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2. Example: in DKA with HCO3 of 10 → expected PaCO2 = 15 + 8 = 23 (range 21–25). If the measured PaCO2 matches, compensation is appropriate. If PaCO2 is HIGHER than expected, there is an extra RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (patient tiring, not breathing off enough CO2 — danger sign). If it is LOWER, there is an extra RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The body compensates by deep, fast Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — In RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS the primary problem is CO2; compensation is by the KIDNEYS (keeping HCO3), so Winter's formula does not apply.
• Metabolic alkalosis — METABOLIC ALKALOSIS uses a different estimate (PaCO2 rises about 0.7 × rise in HCO3), not Winter's formula.
• Respiratory alkalosis — RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS is a primary CO2 problem compensated by the kidneys; Winter's formula is not used.

⭐ Key point: Winter's formula = expected PaCO2 in METABOLIC ACIDOSIS = 1.5 × HCO3 + 8 ± 2.$x$, null),
(18, $x$Culture and sensitivity testing of a clinical specimen is primarily performed to:$x$, $x$Identify the causative organism and determine which antibiotics it is sensitive to, guiding targeted therapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Identify the causative organism and determine which antibiotics it is sensitive to, guiding targeted therapy
CULTURE AND SENSITIVITY (C&S) has two parts. CULTURE: the specimen (blood, urine, sputum, wound swab, CSF) is grown on special media to find out WHICH organism is causing the infection. SENSITIVITY: the organism is tested against different antibiotics to see which ones kill or stop it (sensitive) and which ones it resists (resistant). This guides TARGETED therapy — narrowing from broad-spectrum to the right drug, which improves outcomes and reduces antimicrobial resistance. Nursing points: collect specimens with sterile technique and BEFORE starting antibiotics where possible (without delaying antibiotics in sepsis), label correctly, and send quickly. Results usually take 48–72 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure the patient's haemoglobin level — Haemoglobin is measured by a full blood count (FBC), not by culture.
• Assess kidney function — Kidney function is assessed by urea, creatinine, eGFR and electrolytes, not culture.
• Determine blood type — Blood type is found by blood grouping and cross-matching, not culture.

⭐ Key point: C&S = identify the ORGANISM + find which ANTIBIOTICS work. Take specimens before antibiotics if it causes no delay.$x$, null),
(19, $x$The normal pH range for arterial blood is:$x$, $x$7.35–7.45$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 7.35–7.45
Normal ARTERIAL blood pH is 7.35–7.45. Below 7.35 is ACIDAEMIA (acidosis); above 7.45 is ALKALAEMIA (alkalosis). The body keeps pH in this narrow range because enzymes, heart rhythm and brain function depend on it. Three systems defend pH: chemical buffers (seconds — bicarbonate, proteins, haemoglobin), the LUNGS (minutes — change CO2 by breathing faster or slower), and the KIDNEYS (hours to days — excrete H⁺, keep or lose HCO3⁻). Other normal ABG values: PaCO2 35–45 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 22–26 mmol/L, PaO2 80–100 mmHg, base excess −2 to +2. A pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is usually not compatible with life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7.0–7.2 — 7.0–7.2 is SEVERE ACIDOSIS, seen in conditions like DKA or cardiac arrest — not normal.
• 7.5–7.6 — 7.5–7.6 is ALKALOSIS (e.g. hyperventilation or severe vomiting) — not normal.
• 6.8–7.0 — 6.8–7.0 is life-threatening acidosis, at the edge of survival.

⭐ Key point: Normal arterial pH = 7.35–7.45. PaCO2 35–45; HCO3 22–26.$x$, null),
(20, $x$The normal anion gap range is:$x$, $x$8–18mmol/L$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8–18mmol/L
The ANION GAP (AG) is a calculated value: AG = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻). It represents the "unmeasured" negative ions in the blood (mainly albumin, plus phosphate, sulphate and organic acids). The commonly quoted normal range is about 8–12 (some labs up to 16–18 mmol/L, or 10–20 if potassium is added) — so the best option here is 8–18. A HIGH anion gap metabolic acidosis means extra acid has been added, memory aid "MUDPILES": Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Propylene glycol/Paraldehyde, Isoniazid/Iron, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates. A NORMAL anion gap acidosis is from bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, renal tubular acidosis) and is usually hyperchloraemic. Low albumin lowers the AG, so correct for it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0–5mmol/L — 0–5 mmol/L is too LOW; this is seen with very low albumin or lab error, not normal.
• 30–50mmol/L — 30–50 mmol/L is a very HIGH gap, e.g. severe lactic acidosis or ketoacidosis.
• 100–150mmol/L — 100–150 mmol/L is impossible as an anion gap; it is close to the serum sodium value itself.

⭐ Key point: Anion gap = Na − (Cl + HCO3); normal ≈ 8–18 mmol/L. High AG acidosis = "MUDPILES".$x$, null),
(21, $x$Microcytic anaemia (low MCV) is classically associated with:$x$, $x$Iron deficiency$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Iron deficiency
MCV (mean cell volume) tells us the SIZE of red cells. MICROCYTIC means small cells (MCV under about 80 fL), usually also pale (hypochromic). The commonest cause of microcytic anaemia worldwide is IRON DEFICIENCY — from blood loss (heavy menstruation, hookworm, GI bleeding, ulcers, colon cancer), poor diet, pregnancy or poor absorption. Without iron the cell cannot make enough haemoglobin, so it divides extra times and ends up small. Other microcytic causes: thalassaemia, anaemia of chronic disease (sometimes), sideroblastic anaemia, lead poisoning ("TAILS"). Tests: low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC. Clinical signs: koilonychia (spoon nails), angular stomatitis, pica.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin B12 deficiency — VITAMIN B12 deficiency gives MACROCYTIC (large cell, megaloblastic) anaemia with MCV over 100 fL, and may cause neurological signs.
• Folate deficiency — FOLATE deficiency also gives MACROCYTIC megaloblastic anaemia, not microcytic.
• Acute haemorrhage only — ACUTE HAEMORRHAGE gives a NORMOCYTIC anaemia at first, because the cells lost are normal-sized; microcytosis only appears later after iron stores run out.

⭐ Key point: Microcytic (MCV < 80) = IRON DEFICIENCY first ("TAILS"). Macrocytic = B12/folate. Acute bleed = normocytic.$x$, null),
(22, $x$A rise in PaCO2 causing acidosis indicates the disorder is:$x$, $x$Respiratory$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory
To decide whether an acid–base problem is RESPIRATORY or METABOLIC, look at which value moved in the SAME direction as the pH problem. CO2 acts like an ACID in the blood (CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 → H⁺ + HCO3⁻). So if PaCO2 RISES (above 45 mmHg) and the pH FALLS (below 7.35), the cause is the LUNGS not removing enough CO2 — RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. Causes: hypoventilation from opioid overdose, COPD exacerbation, exhaustion, neuromuscular weakness, head injury. Treatment improves ventilation (stimulate, reverse opioids, NIV/BiPAP, intubation). If the HCO3 had fallen to cause acidosis, it would be metabolic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic — METABOLIC disorders are caused by changes in HCO3⁻ (bicarbonate), not by a rise in CO2.
• Neither respiratory nor metabolic — "Neither" is wrong — a primary change in PaCO2 is by definition a respiratory disorder.
• Always compensated — It is NOT "always compensated"; acute respiratory acidosis is often uncompensated because the kidneys need days to retain bicarbonate.

⭐ Key point: High PaCO2 + low pH = RESPIRATORY acidosis (hypoventilation). Low HCO3 + low pH = metabolic acidosis.$x$, null),
(23, $x$A high anion gap metabolic acidosis, such as in DKA, is caused by:$x$, $x$Extra unmeasured acid (e.g. ketones) in the blood$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Extra unmeasured acid (e.g. ketones) in the blood
In DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA), lack of insulin makes the body burn fat, producing KETOACIDS (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate). These are EXTRA UNMEASURED ACIDS. They release H⁺, which uses up bicarbonate, and their negative ions are not counted in the anion gap formula, so the anion gap RISES. This is called a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Same process with lactic acidosis (sepsis, shock), uraemia, and poisons like methanol, ethylene glycol and aspirin ("MUDPILES"). Treatment of DKA: IV fluids, IV insulin infusion, potassium replacement and monitoring of ketones, glucose and anion gap until it closes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loss of bicarbonate from diarrhoea — Loss of bicarbonate from DIARRHOEA causes a NORMAL anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis, because chloride rises to replace the lost bicarbonate.
• Excess vomiting — Excess VOMITING loses stomach acid (HCl), causing METABOLIC ALKALOSIS, not acidosis.
• Hyperventilation alone — HYPERVENTILATION alone blows off CO2 and causes RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

⭐ Key point: High anion gap acidosis = ADDED acid (ketones, lactate, toxins). Diarrhoea = normal AG acidosis. Vomiting = alkalosis.$x$, null),
(24, $x$A mother is admitted to the emergency department following complaints of fever and chills. The nurse on duty took her vital signs and noted the following: Temp = 100 °F; apical pulse = 95; respiration = 20 and deep. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.37, PaO2 90 mm Hg, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, and HCO3 24 mmol/L. What is your assessment?$x$, $x$Hyperthermia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperthermia
Look at each value. TEMPERATURE 100 °F = about 37.8 °C (°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9). Normal is about 97.7–99.5 °F (36.5–37.5 °C), so 100 °F is a raised temperature — mild HYPERTHERMIA/fever, fitting the "fever and chills" complaint. Pulse 95 and respiration 20 are at the upper end of normal (fever raises both). Now the ABG: pH 7.37 (normal 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 40 (normal 35–45), HCO3 24 (normal 22–26), PaO2 90 (normal 80–100) — ALL NORMAL. So there is no acid–base disorder. The correct assessment is HYPERTHERMIA only. Exam tip: always check every ABG value against its range before naming a disorder; do not guess alkalosis just because breathing is "deep".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothermia — HYPOTHERMIA means a body temperature below 35 °C (95 °F); 100 °F is raised, not low.
• Hypothermia and Respiratory Alkalosis — "Hypothermia and respiratory alkalosis" is wrong twice: the temperature is high and the pH/PaCO2 are normal.
• Hyperthermia and Respiratory Alkalosis — RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS needs pH above 7.45 and PaCO2 below 35; here pH 7.37 and PaCO2 40 are normal.

⭐ Key point: 100 °F ≈ 37.8 °C = hyperthermia. pH 7.37, PaCO2 40, HCO3 24 = NORMAL ABG. Always check each value against its range.$x$, null),
(25, $x$Fibrinogen plays its key role in coagulation by:$x$, $x$Being converted to fibrin to form the structural clot mesh$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Being converted to fibrin to form the structural clot mesh
FIBRINOGEN (clotting factor I) is a soluble protein made by the liver. At the end of the clotting cascade, THROMBIN cuts fibrinogen into FIBRIN. Fibrin strands join together and are cross-linked by factor XIII to form a strong MESH that traps red cells and platelets, turning the soft platelet plug into a firm, stable clot. Normal fibrinogen is about 2–4 g/L. Low fibrinogen (e.g. in DIC, massive haemorrhage, postpartum haemorrhage, liver failure) causes uncontrolled bleeding and is treated with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inhibiting platelet aggregation — Fibrinogen does not INHIBIT platelet aggregation; it actually helps platelets stick together through the GP IIb/IIIa receptor. Drugs like aspirin and clopidogrel inhibit aggregation.
• Breaking down existing clots — BREAKING DOWN clots (fibrinolysis) is done by PLASMIN (activated by tPA), and produces D-dimer from fibrin.
• Regulating serum calcium — Serum calcium is regulated by parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcitonin, not fibrinogen (though calcium is factor IV in clotting).

⭐ Key point: Fibrinogen → (thrombin) → FIBRIN mesh = stable clot. Low fibrinogen → give cryoprecipitate.$x$, null),
(26, $x$Hyperphosphataemia is a common electrolyte disturbance in patients with:$x$, $x$Chronic kidney disease$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic kidney disease
The KIDNEYS are the main route for removing PHOSPHATE from the body. In CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE (CKD), the glomerular filtration rate falls, so phosphate is retained and blood levels rise (HYPERPHOSPHATAEMIA, normal 0.8–1.5 mmol/L). High phosphate binds calcium, so calcium falls; the failing kidney also makes less active vitamin D. Low calcium and high phosphate drive the parathyroid glands to overwork (secondary hyperparathyroidism), causing renal bone disease, itching and calcium–phosphate deposits in vessels (vascular calcification). Management: low-phosphate diet (limit dairy, cola, processed foods), PHOSPHATE BINDERS taken with meals (calcium carbonate, sevelamer), and dialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes insipidus — DIABETES INSIPIDUS causes loss of large amounts of dilute urine and HYPERNATRAEMIA, not high phosphate.
• Primary hyperaldosteronism — PRIMARY HYPERALDOSTERONISM causes HYPOKALAEMIA, sodium retention and hypertension.
• SIADH — SIADH causes water retention and dilutional HYPONATRAEMIA.

⭐ Key point: CKD → high PHOSPHATE + low calcium → secondary hyperparathyroidism. Treat with diet + phosphate binders with meals.$x$, null),
(27, $x$The most common anticoagulant used in blood bank donor bags is:$x$, $x$Tri-sodium citrate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tri-sodium citrate
Blood bank donor bags contain an anticoagulant-preservative such as CPD or CPDA-1 (Citrate, Phosphate, Dextrose, Adenine). The anticoagulant part is TRI-SODIUM CITRATE. Citrate stops clotting by BINDING CALCIUM (calcium is factor IV, needed at many steps of the cascade). The DEXTROSE feeds the red cells, ADENINE helps them make ATP, and PHOSPHATE buffers the pH; CPDA-1 lets whole blood be stored for about 35 days at 2–6 °C. Clinical link: in MASSIVE transfusion, the citrate in many units can bind the patient's own calcium → HYPOCALCAEMIA (tingling, low BP, arrhythmia), so ionised calcium is monitored and calcium is given if low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• EDTA — EDTA (purple-top tube) is used for full blood count samples; it is not used in donor bags because it is toxic in large amounts.
• Heparin — HEPARIN is used for some samples (green tube) and as a drug, but it does not preserve red cells for weeks of storage.
• Double oxalate — DOUBLE OXALATE is an old laboratory anticoagulant for test samples only; it is toxic and never used for transfusion.

⭐ Key point: Donor bags = CITRATE (CPD/CPDA-1) — binds calcium. Massive transfusion → watch for HYPOCALCAEMIA.$x$, null),
(28, $x$The antidote used to reverse benzodiazepine sedation is:$x$, $x$Flumazenil$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Flumazenil
FLUMAZENIL is the specific antidote for BENZODIAZEPINES (diazepam, midazolam, lorazepam). It is a competitive ANTAGONIST at the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, so it quickly reverses sedation and respiratory depression. Dose: 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds, repeat 0.1–0.2 mg each minute up to about 1 mg. CAUTIONS: it can cause SEIZURES in patients on long-term benzodiazepines (sudden withdrawal) or after mixed overdose with tricyclic antidepressants, so it is mainly used to reverse procedural sedation, not routine overdose. Its effect is shorter than many benzodiazepines, so RE-SEDATION can occur — keep monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — NALOXONE reverses OPIOIDS (morphine, heroin, fentanyl), not benzodiazepines.
• Acetylcysteine — ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC) is the antidote for PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) overdose; it replaces glutathione.
• Protamine sulphate — PROTAMINE SULPHATE reverses HEPARIN.

⭐ Key point: Benzodiazepine → FLUMAZENIL; opioid → naloxone; paracetamol → acetylcysteine; heparin → protamine.$x$, null),
(29, $x$Activated charcoal works by which mechanism to reduce systemic absorption of an ingested poison?$x$, $x$It adsorbs the toxin onto its large surface area within the gastrointestinal tract, preventing systemic absorption$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: It adsorbs the toxin onto its large surface area within the gastrointestinal tract, preventing systemic absorption
ACTIVATED CHARCOAL is a fine black powder with a huge surface area (about 1000 m² per gram). Many drugs and toxins stick to its surface — this is called ADSORPTION — while still in the stomach and intestine, so they cannot be absorbed into the blood and are passed out in the stool. It works best when given within about 1 HOUR of ingestion, in a dose of about 50 g for adults (1 g/kg for children). The patient MUST have a protected airway (alert, or intubated) because aspiration into the lungs is dangerous. It does NOT bind well to iron, lithium, alcohols, cyanide, acids, alkalis or hydrocarbons ("PHAILS").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It chemically neutralises acids and alkalis — It does NOT neutralise acids or alkalis; corrosives are not bound well and charcoal is contraindicated in corrosive ingestion.
• It induces vomiting to expel the toxin — It does NOT induce vomiting; that was ipecac, which is no longer recommended.
• It increases intestinal motility to speed transit — It does NOT increase gut motility; in fact it can cause constipation. Laxatives/whole-bowel irrigation are separate treatments.

⭐ Key point: Activated charcoal ADSORBS toxins in the gut. Best within 1 hour; airway must be protected; not for iron, lithium, alcohol, corrosives.$x$, null),
(30, $x$Patients on a continuous insulin infusion in the ICU require blood glucose monitoring at a frequency of approximately:$x$, $x$Hourly, or per institutional protocol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hourly, or per institutional protocol
Patients on a CONTINUOUS IV INSULIN INFUSION (e.g. DKA, HHS, post-cardiac surgery, critical illness) need BLOOD GLUCOSE checked about EVERY HOUR (or as per unit protocol, sometimes every 1–2 hours once stable). IV regular insulin acts within minutes and has a short half-life, so glucose can fall quickly; hourly checks let the nurse adjust the rate to stay in the target range (often 6–10 mmol/L, or 140–180 mg/dL in ICU) and catch HYPOGLYCAEMIA early. Hypoglycaemia in sedated patients may be hidden (no symptoms). Also monitor potassium, because insulin moves potassium into cells and can cause HYPOKALAEMIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Once every 24 hours — Once every 24 hours is far too seldom; dangerous hypo- or hyperglycaemia could develop unnoticed.
• Once weekly — Once weekly makes no sense for an IV insulin infusion; that frequency fits HbA1c or long-term follow-up, not acute care.
• Only if symptomatic — Checking ONLY if symptomatic is unsafe: ICU patients are often sedated and cannot report hypoglycaemia symptoms.

⭐ Key point: IV insulin infusion → glucose check HOURLY (or per protocol). Watch potassium too.$x$, null),
(31, $x$To ensure homogenization when diluting powdered medication in a vial, the nurse should:$x$, $x$Roll the vial gently between the palms.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Roll the vial gently between the palms.
After adding diluent to a powdered drug, the nurse must mix it evenly (homogenise) without damaging it. The correct method is to ROLL THE VIAL GENTLY BETWEEN THE PALMS until all the powder has dissolved and the solution is clear. Rolling mixes the drug without making froth or air bubbles. Many drugs — especially proteins (insulin suspensions, monoclonal antibodies, some antibiotics, vaccines) — can be DAMAGED (denatured) or form foam by vigorous shaking, which can change the dose drawn up. After mixing, check the solution for particles or discolouration, label the vial with date/time/strength if multi-dose, and follow storage instructions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Do nothing after adding the solution to the vial. — Doing NOTHING after adding diluent may leave undissolved powder, giving the wrong dose.
• Invert the vial and let it stand for 1 minute. — Inverting and leaving it to stand for 1 minute does not reliably dissolve the powder or mix the solution evenly.
• Shake the vial vigorously. — SHAKING VIGOROUSLY creates foam and bubbles and may denature protein drugs, so it is avoided.

⭐ Key point: Reconstituted vials: ROLL GENTLY between the palms — do not shake. Check the solution is clear before drawing up.$x$, null),
(32, $x$Gastric lavage is contraindicated in which of the following situations?$x$, $x$Corrosive/acid ingestion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Corrosive/acid ingestion
GASTRIC LAVAGE (stomach washout through a large tube) is now rarely used. It is CONTRAINDICATED in CORROSIVE (acid or alkali) ingestion because: (1) the oesophagus and stomach walls are already burned and weak, so the tube can PERFORATE them; (2) washing can cause the patient to vomit, so the corrosive passes over the oesophagus again, causing more burns, and may be aspirated into the lungs. Other contraindications: hydrocarbon/petroleum ingestion (aspiration pneumonitis), unprotected airway (reduced consciousness without intubation), and bleeding risk. For corrosives: keep NPO, protect the airway, do NOT induce vomiting or neutralise, and arrange early endoscopy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• General sedative overdose within 1 hour — A sedative overdose within 1 hour was the traditional (though now limited) indication for lavage, not a contraindication, if the airway is protected.
• Barbiturate overdose while still conscious — Barbiturate overdose in a conscious patient within the time window is also an older indication, not a contraindication.
• Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose — Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose may still be a situation where lavage was considered; time alone is not the main contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Gastric lavage — NEVER in CORROSIVE (acid/alkali) or hydrocarbon ingestion, or without a protected airway.$x$, null),
(33, $x$A patient develops serotonin syndrome after being started on two serotonergic medications simultaneously. The nurse should recognise this presentation includes:$x$, $x$Agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and autonomic instability$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and autonomic instability
SEROTONIN SYNDROME is caused by too much serotonin activity in the nervous system, often from COMBINING serotonergic drugs: SSRIs/SNRIs, MAO inhibitors, tramadol, pethidine, linezolid, triptans, ondansetron, St John's wort. It usually starts within 24 hours of a new drug or dose change. Classic TRIAD: (1) MENTAL STATUS changes — agitation, confusion, restlessness; (2) AUTONOMIC instability — HYPERTHERMIA, tachycardia, high BP, sweating, dilated pupils, diarrhoea; (3) NEUROMUSCULAR signs — TREMOR, CLONUS (especially inducible ankle clonus), hyperreflexia, muscle rigidity. Treatment: STOP the drugs, supportive care, cooling, benzodiazepines, and cyproheptadine (serotonin antagonist); severe cases need ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia and hypotension with no other findings — BRADYCARDIA and HYPOTENSION are not typical; serotonin syndrome usually causes TACHYCARDIA and hypertension.
• No clinical significance requiring only observation — It is NOT harmless — severe cases cause very high fever, seizures, rhabdomyolysis and death, so it needs urgent action.
• Improved mental status — It causes WORSENING mental status (agitation, confusion), not improvement.

⭐ Key point: Serotonin syndrome = AGITATION + HYPERTHERMIA + TREMOR/CLONUS + autonomic instability. Stop the drugs; give cyproheptadine.$x$, null),
(34, $x$Succinylcholine is contraindicated in a patient with a personal or family history of:$x$, $x$Malignant hyperthermia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Malignant hyperthermia
SUCCINYLCHOLINE (suxamethonium) is a fast-acting depolarising muscle relaxant used for rapid sequence intubation. It is a strong TRIGGER of MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) — an inherited muscle disorder (often in the RYR1 gene) in which triggering drugs (succinylcholine and volatile anaesthetic gases like sevoflurane) cause uncontrolled calcium release in muscle. Signs: rising end-tidal CO2 (often the first sign), masseter/jaw rigidity, tachycardia, very high temperature, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis. Treatment: stop triggers, give DANTROLENE, cool the patient. So a personal or FAMILY history of MH is an absolute contraindication. Other contraindications: HYPERKALAEMIA risk (burns or crush injury after 24–48 h, spinal cord injury, prolonged immobility), and pseudocholinesterase deficiency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Seasonal allergic rhinitis — SEASONAL ALLERGIC RHINITIS does not affect safety of succinylcholine.
• Migraine headaches — MIGRAINE history is not a contraindication.
• Mild intermittent asthma — MILD INTERMITTENT ASTHMA is not a contraindication to succinylcholine (it does not cause much histamine release).

⭐ Key point: Succinylcholine is contraindicated in MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA history (treat MH with DANTROLENE) and in hyperkalaemia risk.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Bactericidal antibiotics work by:$x$, $x$Actively killing bacteria, e.g. by attacking the cell wall$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Actively killing bacteria, e.g. by attacking the cell wall
BACTERICIDAL antibiotics KILL bacteria directly. Many do this by attacking the CELL WALL (beta-lactams such as penicillins, cephalosporins and carbapenems; vancomycin), so the bacterium bursts. Others damage DNA (fluoroquinolones, metronidazole) or cause faulty proteins (aminoglycosides). BACTERIOSTATIC antibiotics only STOP bacteria from growing and multiplying (e.g. tetracyclines, macrolides, clindamycin, sulphonamides), and the immune system then clears them. Bactericidal drugs are preferred in serious infections or weak immunity — e.g. meningitis, endocarditis, neutropenic sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stopping bacterial growth only — "Stopping bacterial growth only" describes BACTERIOSTATIC drugs, not bactericidal ones.
• Only affecting viruses — Antibiotics do NOT work against VIRUSES; viruses need antivirals (e.g. acyclovir, oseltamivir).
• Having no mechanism of action — Every antibiotic has a mechanism of action; "no mechanism" is simply false.

⭐ Key point: BactericiDAL = KILLS (e.g. cell-wall attack by penicillins). BacterioSTATIC = STOPS growth (e.g. tetracyclines, macrolides).$x$, null),
(36, $x$Ipratropium bromide is used in the management of:$x$, $x$Acute asthmatic attack$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute asthmatic attack
IPRATROPIUM BROMIDE (Atrovent) is an inhaled ANTICHOLINERGIC (antimuscarinic) bronchodilator. It blocks acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors in the airway smooth muscle, which RELAXES the bronchi and reduces mucus secretion. In an ACUTE ASTHMA ATTACK it is given by nebuliser (e.g. 500 micrograms) together with salbutamol for moderate–severe or life-threatening attacks; the combination gives better bronchodilation than salbutamol alone. It is also widely used in COPD. Side effects: dry mouth, urinary retention, and blurred vision/glaucoma if mist gets into the eyes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute gastroenteritis — ACUTE GASTROENTERITIS is treated with fluids/ORS; ipratropium has no role.
• Acute peptic ulcer disease — PEPTIC ULCER disease is treated with PPIs, H2 blockers and H. pylori eradication, not inhaled ipratropium.
• Acute bronchopneumonia — ACUTE BRONCHOPNEUMONIA needs antibiotics, oxygen and chest care; ipratropium is not the treatment for infection (unless there is bronchospasm from co-existing COPD/asthma).

⭐ Key point: Ipratropium = inhaled ANTICHOLINERGIC bronchodilator, given with salbutamol in ACUTE ASTHMA and COPD.$x$, null),
(37, $x$Salbutamol relieves bronchospasm mainly by acting as a:$x$, $x$Beta-2 adrenergic agonist causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Beta-2 adrenergic agonist causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation
SALBUTAMOL (albuterol) is a short-acting BETA-2 ADRENERGIC AGONIST (SABA). Beta-2 receptors are found in bronchial smooth muscle. When salbutamol stimulates them, cAMP rises and the smooth muscle RELAXES, so the airways open (BRONCHODILATION) within minutes; effect lasts 4–6 hours. It is the first-line "reliever" for acute asthma and bronchospasm. Side effects come from beta stimulation: TACHYCARDIA, palpitations, fine TREMOR, anxiety and HYPOKALAEMIA (it drives potassium into cells — this is also why nebulised salbutamol can be used to lower high potassium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic that dries secretions — ANTICHOLINERGICS (e.g. ipratropium) work by blocking muscarinic receptors and drying secretions — that is not how salbutamol works.
• Corticosteroid reducing inflammation only — CORTICOSTEROIDS (e.g. hydrocortisone, prednisolone, budesonide) reduce inflammation over hours; salbutamol is not a steroid.
• Mucolytic that thins mucus — MUCOLYTICS (e.g. acetylcysteine, carbocisteine) thin mucus; salbutamol does not.

⭐ Key point: Salbutamol = BETA-2 AGONIST → bronchial smooth muscle relaxation. Side effects: tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia.$x$, null),
(38, $x$Dopamine's haemodynamic effect varies by dose, with higher doses primarily producing:$x$, $x$Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction
DOPAMINE acts on different receptors at different doses: LOW dose (about 1–5 mcg/kg/min) — dopamine receptors, causing renal and mesenteric vasodilation (the old "renal dose", now not recommended to protect kidneys); MEDIUM dose (about 5–10 mcg/kg/min) — BETA-1 receptors, increasing heart rate and contractility (cardiac output); HIGH dose (above about 10 mcg/kg/min) — ALPHA-1 receptors, causing VASOCONSTRICTION and raised systemic vascular resistance and BP. So the higher-dose effect is ALPHA-ADRENERGIC VASOCONSTRICTION. Nursing: give through a central line if possible (extravasation causes tissue necrosis — antidote phentolamine), monitor BP, heart rate and arrhythmias, and urine output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal vasodilation only — RENAL VASODILATION occurs at LOW doses, not high doses.
• Bronchodilation — BRONCHODILATION is a beta-2 effect, which is not a main action of dopamine.
• Pure beta-2 effects — Dopamine has no "pure beta-2" effect; its beta effect is mainly beta-1 (heart).

⭐ Key point: Dopamine: low = renal/dopaminergic; medium = beta-1 (heart); HIGH = ALPHA-1 vasoconstriction.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Before administering any weight-based critical care drug infusion, best practice is to:$x$, $x$Have the calculation independently double-checked by a second nurse/pharmacist$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Have the calculation independently double-checked by a second nurse/pharmacist
Critical care infusions (e.g. noradrenaline, dopamine, heparin, insulin, potassium, sedatives) are HIGH-ALERT medications: a small calculation error can cause severe harm or death. Weight-based doses (mcg/kg/min) need several steps — weight, concentration, dose, pump rate — and each step can go wrong (decimal errors, wrong units, mg vs mcg). Best practice is an INDEPENDENT DOUBLE CHECK by a second nurse or pharmacist, who does the calculation separately without seeing the first result, then both compare. Also check the right patient, drug, dose, route, time and the pump programming (using smart pumps with drug libraries where available), and document both checkers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skip verification if the nurse is experienced — Skipping checks because a nurse is "experienced" is unsafe; experienced staff also make errors, especially when busy or tired.
• Round the dose up for safety margin — ROUNDING the dose UP is not safe; it can cause overdose. Doses must be exact or follow approved protocols.
• Administer without checking the infusion pump rate — Giving without checking the PUMP RATE ignores a common source of error (wrong rate programmed).

⭐ Key point: High-alert weight-based infusions need an INDEPENDENT DOUBLE CHECK of the calculation AND the pump rate.$x$, null),
(40, $x$Which class of these drugs is contraindicated in patients with high bleeding tendencies?$x$, $x$NSAIDS$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: NSAIDS
Non-selective NSAIDs (ibuprofen, diclofenac, naproxen, aspirin) block both COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes. Blocking COX-1 stops platelets from making THROMBOXANE A2, so PLATELETS CANNOT CLUMP well — bleeding time increases. COX-1 also protects the stomach lining, so NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers and GI BLEEDING. This combination makes NSAIDs contraindicated (or used with great caution) in patients with bleeding disorders, thrombocytopenia, active bleeding, peptic ulcers or on anticoagulants. Paracetamol or opioids are safer choices for pain in these patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COX 2 selective NSAIDS — COX-2 SELECTIVE NSAIDs (e.g. celecoxib) spare COX-1, so they have much less effect on platelets and the stomach; they are not the class most linked to bleeding (though they raise cardiovascular risk).
• Opioids — OPIOIDS do not affect platelets or clotting; they are often used for pain when NSAIDs are unsafe.
• Adjuvants — ADJUVANT analgesics (e.g. amitriptyline, gabapentin) do not usually cause bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Non-selective NSAIDs block COX-1 → poor platelet function + GI ulcers → AVOID in bleeding tendency.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Severe/major reactions to contrast media can include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Improved renal perfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improved renal perfusion
Contrast media (iodinated contrast for CT, angiography) can cause reactions. MILD: warmth, nausea, itching, mild urticaria. MODERATE: widespread hives, vomiting, bronchospasm, facial swelling. SEVERE (anaphylactoid): LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (airway closure), severe bronchospasm, RESPIRATORY ARREST, shock, CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIA and cardiac arrest. Treatment of severe reactions: stop contrast, call for help, airway, oxygen, IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1:1000), IV fluids, antihistamine and steroid. Contrast can also cause CONTRAST-INDUCED NEPHROPATHY (worsened kidney function, a rise in creatinine 48–72 h later) — it does NOT improve renal perfusion. Risk is reduced by checking creatinine, hydrating, and holding metformin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Laryngeal oedema — LARYNGEAL OEDEMA IS a severe reaction — it can block the airway.
• Cardiac arrhythmia — CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIA IS a severe reaction, especially in anaphylactoid shock.
• Respiratory arrest — RESPIRATORY ARREST IS a severe reaction — the end point of airway/bronchospasm problems.

⭐ Key point: Severe contrast reaction = laryngeal oedema, bronchospasm, arrhythmia, arrest → IM adrenaline. Contrast can HARM the kidneys, never improve them.$x$, null),
(42, $x$CT head findings suggestive of raised intracranial pressure include:$x$, $x$Midline shift and effacement (compression) of the ventricles$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Midline shift and effacement (compression) of the ventricles
In RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP), something takes up extra space inside the rigid skull (bleed, tumour, swelling). On CT head this shows as MASS EFFECT: MIDLINE SHIFT (the brain is pushed to one side, a shift over 5 mm is significant), EFFACEMENT (squashing) of the lateral ventricles, loss of sulci (the grooves on the brain surface are flattened by swollen brain), effacement of the basal cisterns, loss of grey–white matter differentiation in diffuse oedema, and signs of herniation. These findings warn of the risk of brain herniation and need urgent neurosurgical review and ICP-lowering measures (head up 30°, normal CO2, osmotherapy such as mannitol or hypertonic saline).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Symmetric ventricles with clear sulci — SYMMETRIC ventricles and CLEAR sulci are NORMAL findings — no pressure effect.
• Normal grey-white matter differentiation only — Normal grey–white differentiation is a normal finding; LOSS of it suggests oedema.
• Absence of any mass effect — ABSENCE of mass effect means no space-occupying pressure, the opposite of raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP on CT = MIDLINE SHIFT, compressed ventricles, effaced sulci/cisterns.$x$, null),
(43, $x$The mnemonic IPPR, used to check chest X-ray quality before interpretation, stands for:$x$, $x$Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation
Before reading a chest X-ray, first check if the film is technically adequate, using IPPR: (1) INSPIRATION — about 5–6 anterior ribs or 9–10 posterior ribs should be seen above the diaphragm; poor inspiration makes the heart look big and the lung bases look hazy. (2) POSITION/PROJECTION — PA (standard) or AP (portable/bedside, which magnifies the heart). (3) PENETRATION — the vertebrae should be just visible behind the heart; over-penetrated films look too dark, under-penetrated too white. (4) ROTATION — the spinous processes should be midway between the medial ends of the clavicles; rotation distorts the mediastinum. Many teachers add identity, date and side markers first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report — "Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report" is not the standard meaning of IPPR (identity is checked separately).
• Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate — "Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate" relates to ventilation/haemodynamics, not X-ray quality.
• Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result — "Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result" wrongly includes perfusion, which an X-ray cannot assess.

⭐ Key point: CXR quality = IPPR: Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation.$x$, null),
(44, $x$Positive contrast agents (e.g. iodine, barium) appear on X-ray as:$x$, $x$Opaque/white$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Opaque/white
On X-ray, the WHITER an area, the MORE X-rays it has blocked. POSITIVE contrast agents like IODINE and BARIUM have HIGH atomic numbers, so they absorb many X-rays and look OPAQUE/WHITE (radio-OPAQUE), similar to bone or metal. This lets us outline the inside of hollow structures: barium swallow or enema for the gut, iodine for blood vessels (angiography), urinary tract (IVU) or CT enhancement. NEGATIVE contrast agents (air, CO2) absorb few X-rays and look DARK. Nursing: ask about iodine allergy and previous reactions, check kidney function before iodinated contrast, and give fluids/laxatives after barium to prevent constipation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dark/lucent — DARK/LUCENT appearance is seen with AIR or negative contrast, not iodine/barium.
• Invisible — They are certainly not invisible — they are designed to be clearly seen.
• Same density as air — Air density is BLACK on X-ray; positive contrast is the opposite (white).

⭐ Key point: Positive contrast (iodine, barium) = WHITE/radio-opaque. Air/negative contrast = black.$x$, null),
(45, $x$The three core principles for minimising radiation exposure during a radiological incident are:$x$, $x$Time, distance, and shielding$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Time, distance, and shielding
There are three key principles to reduce RADIATION EXPOSURE (ALARA — As Low As Reasonably Achievable): (1) TIME — spend as LITTLE time as possible near the radiation source; dose increases with time. (2) DISTANCE — stay as FAR away as possible; radiation falls with the SQUARE of the distance (inverse square law), so doubling the distance cuts exposure to one quarter. (3) SHIELDING — put a barrier between you and the source, e.g. lead aprons, thyroid collars, lead screens, concrete walls. Nurses use these when patients have portable X-rays (step back about 2 metres or behind a screen) or have received radioactive implants.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Speed, volume, and pressure — Speed, volume and pressure are not radiation protection principles.
• Triage, treatment, and transport — Triage, treatment and transport are steps in managing a mass casualty incident, not principles of reducing radiation dose.
• Isolation, ventilation, and hydration — Isolation, ventilation and hydration are not the three core principles for radiation protection.

⭐ Key point: Radiation protection = TIME (less), DISTANCE (more — inverse square law), SHIELDING (lead).$x$, null),
(46, $x$Sharing your personal EHR login credentials with a colleague is:$x$, $x$A breach of data confidentiality and accountability$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A breach of data confidentiality and accountability
Each staff member has their OWN Electronic Health Record (EHR) login so that every entry and every record viewed is linked to a named person (an AUDIT TRAIL). Sharing your username/password with a colleague: (1) breaches PATIENT CONFIDENTIALITY — unauthorised people may see records; (2) breaks ACCOUNTABILITY — any wrong entry, missed drug or data theft will be recorded under YOUR name; (3) breaks hospital policy and data protection law (e.g. Nigeria Data Protection Act, professional code of conduct), and can lead to disciplinary action. Always log out or lock the screen when leaving a terminal, never write down passwords, and report any suspected misuse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acceptable if they are busy — It is NOT acceptable even if the colleague is busy; they must use their own login or get one issued.
• Required by hospital policy — No hospital policy REQUIRES sharing passwords; policies forbid it.
• Only a problem if a wrong entry is made — It is a problem even if no wrong entry is made; the breach happens at the moment of sharing.

⭐ Key point: Never share EHR login details — it breaches CONFIDENTIALITY and ACCOUNTABILITY. Lock the screen when you leave.$x$, null),
(47, $x$Which of the following is an example of internet browser?$x$, $x$Internet explorer$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Internet explorer
A WEB (INTERNET) BROWSER is a software application used to open and view websites on the internet. INTERNET EXPLORER (by Microsoft, now replaced by Microsoft Edge) is a classic example. Other browsers: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Safari, Opera. Nurses use browsers to access online drug references, guidelines, e-learning and web-based hospital systems. Basic computer knowledge is tested in nursing informatics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Microsoft Word — MICROSOFT WORD is a WORD-PROCESSING program for writing documents, not a browser.
• Notepad — NOTEPAD is a simple TEXT EDITOR for plain text, not a browser.
• Internet navigator — "Internet navigator" is not a real browser name; the old browser was "Netscape Navigator", so this option is a distractor.

⭐ Key point: Web browser examples: Internet Explorer/Edge, Chrome, Firefox, Safari. Word = word processor; Notepad = text editor.$x$, null),
(48, $x$A nurse is using an Electronic Health Record (EHR) to access a patient's lab results. The EHR system provides a centralized location for patient data, which helps to:$x$, $x$Improve continuity of care and coordination among the healthcare team$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improve continuity of care and coordination among the healthcare team
An ELECTRONIC HEALTH RECORD (EHR) keeps a patient's information — history, medications, allergies, lab results, imaging, care plans, notes — in ONE CENTRAL digital place that all authorised members of the team can access at the same time, from different departments. This IMPROVES CONTINUITY OF CARE (each shift and specialist can see what has been done) and COORDINATION among doctors, nurses, pharmacists and others. Other benefits: legible records, quicker access to results, drug-interaction alerts, fewer duplicated tests, and better data for audit and research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the time it takes to retrieve patient data — EHRs DECREASE, not increase, the time needed to retrieve patient data.
• Ensure each department uses its own unique record system — The aim is ONE SHARED record, not a separate system for each department (that causes fragmented care).
• Eliminate the need for any verbal communication between nurses — EHRs do NOT replace verbal communication; handover and direct communication between nurses remain essential for safety.

⭐ Key point: EHR = one central record shared by the team → better CONTINUITY and COORDINATION of care.$x$, null),
(49, $x$If a nurse suspects a patient data breach has occurred, the appropriate first action is to:$x$, $x$Report it immediately according to institutional policy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Report it immediately according to institutional policy
A DATA BREACH is any unauthorised access, loss or sharing of patient information (e.g. a lost laptop, records seen by someone with no reason, a misdirected email). The nurse's first action is to REPORT IT IMMEDIATELY through the proper channel — usually the nurse in charge/manager and the hospital's information governance or data protection officer — following institutional policy. Early reporting lets the hospital contain the breach, protect the patient, and meet legal duties to inform regulators and affected people within set times. Document the facts and do not try to cover it up.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it if no harm seems obvious — IGNORING it is wrong; harm may not be obvious yet, and hiding a breach is itself a disciplinary and legal offence.
• Delete the affected records — DELETING records destroys evidence and the patient's clinical record — this is illegal and unsafe.
• Discuss it publicly with colleagues on social media — Discussing it on SOCIAL MEDIA spreads the breach further and is unprofessional.

⭐ Key point: Suspected data breach → REPORT IMMEDIATELY per policy. Never ignore, delete or post about it.$x$, null),
(50, $x$CT scan and ultrasound machines are best classified as which type of healthcare ICT device?$x$, $x$Diagnostic devices$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diagnostic devices
Healthcare ICT (information and communication technology) devices are grouped by what they do. DIAGNOSTIC devices help identify disease by producing images or results — CT scanners, MRI, ULTRASOUND, X-ray, ECG machines, laboratory analysers. MONITORING devices continuously watch the patient's condition — cardiac monitors, pulse oximeters, ventilator alarms, ICP monitors. TREATMENT (therapeutic) devices deliver therapy — infusion pumps, ventilators, dialysis machines, defibrillators. COMMUNICATION devices share information — phones, pagers, nurse call systems, telemedicine links. CT and ultrasound make images to help diagnose, so they are DIAGNOSTIC devices.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitoring devices only — MONITORING devices track vital signs over time; CT and ultrasound are not used for continuous monitoring.
• Treatment devices only — TREATMENT devices deliver therapy; CT and ultrasound mainly diagnose (ultrasound can guide procedures but that is not its classification).
• Communication devices — COMMUNICATION devices transmit messages; imaging machines are not in this group.

⭐ Key point: CT, ultrasound, MRI, X-ray = DIAGNOSTIC devices.$x$, null),
(51, $x$The target SpO₂ in acute severe asthma treatment is:$x$, $x$93–95%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 93–95%
In ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA, the British Thoracic Society/SIGN guideline says give controlled oxygen to keep SpO2 at 94–98%; many exam sources (and this question) quote a target of about 93–95%. The point is: give enough oxygen to correct hypoxaemia, but avoid aiming for 100%, because high-flow oxygen can mask deterioration and unnecessary hyperoxia has no benefit. Oxygen is used to drive nebulised salbutamol in asthma. Life-threatening asthma signs: SpO2 under 92%, PEF under 33%, silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, confusion, normal or rising PaCO2 — these need urgent senior/ICU review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100% — 100% is not the target; excessive oxygen gives no extra benefit and may hide worsening.
• 97–99% — 97–99% is higher than needed; the guideline target range is lower.
• 85–90% — 85–90% is too LOW for asthma; it is close to the COPD/CO2-retainer range (88–92%) and means dangerous hypoxaemia in asthma.

⭐ Key point: Acute asthma: target SpO2 about 93–95% (BTS 94–98%). SpO2 below 92% = life-threatening.$x$, null),
(52, $x$A ventilated patient's SpO2 drops from 96% to 88% acutely. Using a systematic differential for displacement, obstruction, pneumothorax, and equipment failure, the nurse should assess for all of these EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Recent oral care$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Recent oral care
When a ventilated patient suddenly desaturates, use the DOPE mnemonic: DISPLACEMENT of the tube (slipped into the right main bronchus or out of the trachea), OBSTRUCTION of the tube (secretions, blood, kinking, biting), PNEUMOTHORAX (especially tension pneumothorax from high pressures), EQUIPMENT failure (ventilator, oxygen supply, disconnected circuit). Immediate action: disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen with a bag-valve device, check tube position and breath sounds, pass a suction catheter, look at chest movement, check the equipment. RECENT ORAL CARE is part of routine VAP prevention and is not one of the causes in this emergency differential (though careless oral care can move the tube, which is "displacement").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tube displacement or dislodgement — Tube DISPLACEMENT is the "D" in DOPE and must be checked.
• Airway obstruction from secretions — Airway OBSTRUCTION from secretions is the "O" in DOPE — suction is a key step.
• Pneumothorax — PNEUMOTHORAX is the "P" in DOPE; a tension pneumothorax needs immediate decompression.

⭐ Key point: Sudden desaturation on a ventilator = DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment.$x$, null),
(53, $x$What percentage of chest injuries are managed WITHOUT surgery?$x$, $x$80%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 80%
Most CHEST INJURIES — about 80–85% (often quoted as "over 80%" or "85–90%") — are managed WITHOUT major surgery (thoracotomy). The usual treatments are simple: oxygen, good PAIN RELIEF (so the patient can breathe deeply and cough), physiotherapy, and CHEST DRAIN (intercostal tube) insertion for pneumothorax or haemothorax. Only about 10–20% need thoracotomy — for example, massive haemothorax (over 1500 mL immediately or over 200 mL/hour), cardiac tamponade not relieved, major airway or great vessel injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20% — 20% is far too low; it would mean most chest injuries need surgery, which is not true.
• 50% — 50% is also too low.
• 100% — 100% is impossible — some chest injuries (e.g. massive bleeding, great vessel injury) definitely need surgery.

⭐ Key point: About 80% of chest injuries need NO thoracotomy — just oxygen, analgesia, physiotherapy and chest drains.$x$, null),
(54, $x$Inhaled nitric oxide is used in pulmonary hypertensive crisis primarily because it:$x$, $x$Selectively dilates the pulmonary vasculature with minimal systemic hypotension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Selectively dilates the pulmonary vasculature with minimal systemic hypotension
INHALED NITRIC OXIDE (iNO) is a gas given through the ventilator circuit (usually 5–20 ppm). Because it is breathed in, it reaches only the pulmonary vessels next to VENTILATED alveoli, where it relaxes vascular smooth muscle (through cGMP), DILATING the pulmonary arteries. This lowers PULMONARY artery pressure and improves V/Q matching (better oxygenation). It is quickly inactivated by haemoglobin in the blood, so it has very little effect on SYSTEMIC BP — it is a SELECTIVE pulmonary vasodilator. Uses: pulmonary hypertensive crisis (e.g. after cardiac surgery), persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, right heart failure. Monitor methaemoglobin and NO2 levels; do not stop it suddenly (rebound pulmonary hypertension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increases systemic vascular resistance — iNO does NOT increase systemic vascular resistance; it is a vasodilator.
• Acts as a potent bronchoconstrictor — It is a BRONCHODILATOR/vasodilator effect, not a bronchoconstrictor.
• Directly increases myocardial contractility — It does not directly increase myocardial contractility; it helps the right ventricle by lowering its afterload.

⭐ Key point: Inhaled NO = SELECTIVE PULMONARY VASODILATOR — lowers pulmonary pressure with little systemic hypotension. Wean slowly.$x$, null),
(55, $x$The most common complication following thoracotomy is:$x$, $x$Atelectasis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atelectasis
After THORACOTOMY (opening the chest), the commonest complication is ATELECTASIS — collapse of alveoli/lung segments. Reasons: severe incision pain makes the patient breathe shallowly and avoid coughing; anaesthesia and opioids reduce deep breaths; secretions build up and block small airways. Atelectasis causes low SpO2, fever in the first 1–2 days and can lead to pneumonia. Prevention is a key nursing task: good PAIN CONTROL (epidural/PCA), early mobilisation, deep breathing exercises, incentive spirometry, supported coughing (splinting the wound with a pillow), chest physiotherapy, and managing chest drains.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wound dehiscence — WOUND DEHISCENCE (wound opening) is possible but uncommon after thoracotomy.
• Bronchopleural fistula — BRONCHOPLEURAL FISTULA is a serious complication (mainly after pneumonectomy/lobectomy) but much less common.
• Pericardial tamponade — PERICARDIAL TAMPONADE is rare after thoracotomy; it is more associated with cardiac surgery.

⭐ Key point: Commonest post-thoracotomy complication = ATELECTASIS. Prevent with pain control, deep breathing, coughing, early mobilisation.$x$, null),
(56, $x$A patient with respiratory failure is receiving oxygenation and ventilation support. The nurse knows that the main goal of this therapy is to:$x$, $x$Maintain adequate gas exchange and support vital organ function$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Maintain adequate gas exchange and support vital organ function
RESPIRATORY FAILURE means the lungs cannot keep normal blood gases: Type 1 — low PaO2 (below 60 mmHg/8 kPa) with normal or low CO2; Type 2 — low PaO2 plus high PaCO2 (above 50 mmHg/6.5 kPa). Oxygen therapy and ventilation (NIV or mechanical) are SUPPORTIVE: their main goal is to MAINTAIN ADEQUATE GAS EXCHANGE (oxygenation and CO2 removal) so that the brain, heart, kidneys and other VITAL ORGANS keep working, while the medical team treats the cause and the lungs recover. They "buy time".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Keep the patient asleep and comfortable — Keeping the patient asleep and comfortable is a part of sedation management, not the main goal of ventilation.
• Reduce the workload of the heart — Ventilation may reduce the work of BREATHING and can affect the heart, but reducing cardiac workload is not the main aim.
• Reverse the underlying cause of the respiratory failure — Ventilation itself does NOT reverse the cause (e.g. pneumonia needs antibiotics, asthma needs bronchodilators); it supports the patient while the cause is treated.

⭐ Key point: Goal of oxygen/ventilation in respiratory failure = maintain ADEQUATE GAS EXCHANGE and protect vital organs while the cause is treated.$x$, null),
(57, $x$Non-invasive ventilation (NIV) such as BiPAP is generally contraindicated in a patient who:$x$, $x$Has a reduced level of consciousness and cannot protect their airway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Has a reduced level of consciousness and cannot protect their airway
NIV (BiPAP/CPAP) uses a tight face or nasal mask; the patient must be able to breathe on their own, cooperate, and PROTECT THEIR AIRWAY (cough, swallow, clear secretions). A patient with REDUCED CONSCIOUSNESS (e.g. GCS below about 8, not due to CO2 narcosis that may quickly improve) cannot protect the airway, so there is a high risk of vomiting and ASPIRATION into the lungs under the mask; such patients need intubation. Other contraindications: respiratory arrest, haemodynamic instability, facial trauma/burns, recent upper GI or facial surgery, copious secretions, vomiting, undrained pneumothorax, inability to fit the mask.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has mild COPD exacerbation with good cooperation — A MILD COPD exacerbation in a cooperative patient is actually a classic INDICATION (especially with hypercapnic acidosis).
• Is able to tolerate the mask comfortably — Being able to TOLERATE THE MASK is needed for NIV to work — it supports use, not contraindicates it.
• Has stable haemodynamics — STABLE HAEMODYNAMICS is a requirement for safe NIV, not a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: NIV needs an alert, cooperative patient who can protect their airway. Reduced consciousness = contraindication → intubate.$x$, null),
(58, $x$A patient with pulmonary fibrosis has restrictive lung disease. On spirometry, the nurse would expect:$x$, $x$Reduced FEV1 with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, and reduced total lung capacity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reduced FEV1 with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, and reduced total lung capacity
In RESTRICTIVE lung disease (pulmonary fibrosis, chest wall deformity, obesity, neuromuscular weakness) the lungs cannot EXPAND fully. Both FEV1 (air blown out in 1 second) and FVC (total air blown out) are REDUCED, but they fall roughly together, so the FEV1/FVC RATIO is NORMAL or even HIGH (above 70–80%, because stiff lungs empty quickly). TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY is REDUCED — this is the hallmark of restriction. Gas transfer (DLCO) is also reduced in fibrosis. In OBSTRUCTIVE disease (asthma, COPD) FEV1 falls much more than FVC, so the ratio is LOW (below 0.7), and TLC is normal or increased (air trapping).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased total lung capacity with a low FEV1/FVC ratio — High TLC with a LOW FEV1/FVC ratio describes OBSTRUCTIVE disease such as emphysema, not fibrosis.
• Normal spirometry results in all cases — Spirometry is NOT normal in significant restrictive disease; volumes are reduced.
• Increased FVC with normal FEV1 — FVC is DECREASED, not increased, in restrictive disease.

⭐ Key point: Restrictive = low FEV1 and FVC, NORMAL/HIGH ratio, LOW TLC. Obstructive = LOW ratio (<0.7), high TLC.$x$, null),
(59, $x$In patients with respiratory distress, damage to type II alveolar cells can impair which of the following key functions?$x$, $x$Secretion of surfactant$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Secretion of surfactant
The alveolar wall has two main cell types. TYPE I cells are thin and flat, covering about 95% of the surface and forming the gas exchange barrier. TYPE II cells are rounder and make SURFACTANT — a mixture of phospholipids and proteins that lowers SURFACE TENSION in the alveoli, stopping them from collapsing at the end of expiration and making the lungs easier to inflate. Type II cells also act as stem cells to repair type I cells. Damage to type II cells (as in ARDS, or immaturity in premature babies — neonatal respiratory distress syndrome) means less surfactant, so alveoli collapse (atelectasis), lungs become stiff and hypoxaemia worsens.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immunity — IMMUNITY in the alveoli is mainly provided by alveolar MACROPHAGES, not type II cells.
• Capacitation — CAPACITATION is a process in which SPERM become able to fertilise an egg; it has nothing to do with the lungs.
• Gaseous exchange — Gas exchange happens mainly across TYPE I cells; type II damage affects it indirectly, but the KEY specific type II function is surfactant secretion.

⭐ Key point: Type II alveolar cells = SURFACTANT (prevents collapse). Type I cells = gas exchange surface.$x$, null),
(60, $x$In which mode of ventilator the patient will initiate his/her own breath and does not depend on ventilator. Ventilator will only assess the patient efforts.$x$, $x$Spontaneous mood$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous mood
In SPONTANEOUS mode (e.g. CPAP or Pressure Support/PSV), the PATIENT starts EVERY breath and controls the rate and depth; the ventilator does not give mandatory breaths. It only senses the patient's effort (trigger) and gives support — a set pressure (pressure support) and/or PEEP — to make breathing easier. There is no backup rate unless apnoea ventilation is set, so it is used for alert patients with a good respiratory drive, especially during WEANING (spontaneous breathing trials).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AC mood — ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) gives a set number of full mandatory breaths; every patient-triggered breath also gets a full set breath, so the patient depends on the ventilator.
• SIMV mood — SIMV gives a set number of mandatory breaths synchronised with the patient; between them the patient can breathe spontaneously — but it still delivers mandatory breaths.
• VCV mood — VCV (volume-controlled ventilation) delivers a set tidal volume at a set rate — a controlled mode, not patient-driven.

⭐ Key point: SPONTANEOUS mode (CPAP/PSV): patient starts all breaths; ventilator only supports — used for weaning.$x$, null),
(61, $x$Using the ABCDE framework to prioritise care for a newly admitted critically ill patient, which finding would take priority over the others?$x$, $x$Stridor with visible respiratory distress$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stridor with visible respiratory distress
The ABCDE approach (Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure) puts problems in order of how quickly they kill. AIRWAY comes first: a blocked airway can cause death within minutes. STRIDOR is a high-pitched noise on breathing in, caused by narrowing of the upper airway (larynx/trachea) — e.g. swelling, foreign body, anaphylaxis, burns, epiglottitis. Stridor PLUS visible respiratory distress means the airway is in danger now. Action: call for senior/anaesthetic help immediately, sit the patient up, give high-flow oxygen, prepare for airway management (adrenaline nebuliser if croup/anaphylaxis, intubation or surgical airway).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild abrasions on the forearm — Mild forearm ABRASIONS belong to E (Exposure) — the lowest priority here.
• A blood pressure of 128/82 — A BP of 128/82 is a near-normal circulation finding — no urgent action.
• A pain score of 3/10 — A pain score of 3/10 is mild pain; it should be treated but is not life-threatening.

⭐ Key point: ABCDE: AIRWAY first. Stridor + respiratory distress = threatened airway → highest priority.$x$, null),
(62, $x$In status asthmaticus, oxygen is given to keep the patient's SpO2 at:$x$, $x$Greater than 92%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Greater than 92%
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS (severe asthma attack not responding to usual treatment), the patient is hypoxic because of bronchospasm, mucus plugging and V/Q mismatch. Oxygen should be given to keep SpO2 ABOVE 92% (guidelines: 94–98%). Low oxygen in asthma is a sign of LIFE-THREATENING severity, and a falling SpO2 means the patient is tiring. Other treatment: nebulised salbutamol (back-to-back) with ipratropium, systemic steroids, IV magnesium sulphate, and ICU review for possible intubation if exhausted or PaCO2 rising.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 75% — 75% is severe, dangerous hypoxaemia — far too low.
• 88% — 88% is the lower target used for COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention, not for asthma.
• Exactly 90% — "Exactly 90%" is too low and too rigid; a target range above 92% is used.

⭐ Key point: Status asthmaticus: keep SpO2 > 92% (94–98%). SpO2 < 92% = life-threatening.$x$, null),
(63, $x$While monitoring during ventilator support, which one of the following is NOT a responsibility of nurse;$x$, $x$Change ventilator setting frequently$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Change ventilator setting frequently
During mechanical ventilation the NURSE's responsibilities include: monitoring the patient (SpO2, vital signs, chest movement, breath sounds, comfort, sedation level), checking for AIR LEAKS (cuff pressure 20–30 cmH2O, circuit connections), SUCTIONING when there are secretions (as needed, not routinely), checking and responding to alarms, collecting/assisting with ABG samples and reporting results, oral care and VAP prevention, and accurate documentation. CHANGING VENTILATOR SETTINGS is NOT a routine nursing task — settings (FiO2, PEEP, mode, rate, tidal volume) are ordered by the doctor/intensivist (or respiratory therapist per protocol), and changing them "frequently" without orders would be unsafe and unstable for the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Check any indications of air leak — Checking for AIR LEAKS IS a nursing responsibility (cuff pressure, circuit).
• Do suctioning if needed — SUCTIONING as needed IS a nursing responsibility.
• Arterial blood gas measurement — ARTERIAL BLOOD GAS sampling/monitoring IS part of nursing care (taking samples from an arterial line and reporting results).

⭐ Key point: Nurses monitor, suction, check leaks and ABGs — they do NOT change ventilator settings without orders.$x$, null),
(64, $x$Stroke volume is defined as:$x$, $x$The volume of blood ejected by the ventricle with each heartbeat$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The volume of blood ejected by the ventricle with each heartbeat
STROKE VOLUME (SV) is the volume of blood ejected by the VENTRICLE with EACH heartbeat. Normal is about 60–100 mL (about 70 mL average). It is found by: SV = end-diastolic volume − end-systolic volume. CARDIAC OUTPUT = SV × heart rate (about 70 mL × 70 beats/min ≈ 5 L/min). Stroke volume depends on three things: PRELOAD (filling — how much the ventricle is stretched), AFTERLOAD (resistance the ventricle pumps against) and CONTRACTILITY (strength of contraction). In shock, SV falls; nurses support it with fluids (preload), vasodilators (afterload) or inotropes (contractility).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The total blood volume in the body — Total blood volume in the body is about 5 litres (70 mL/kg) — not stroke volume.
• The heart rate over one minute — Heart rate over one minute is the PULSE/heart rate, not the volume pumped.
• The pressure generated during diastole — Pressure during diastole is the DIASTOLIC blood pressure, not stroke volume.

⭐ Key point: Stroke volume = blood ejected per beat (60–100 mL). CO = SV × HR. SV depends on preload, afterload, contractility.$x$, null),
(65, $x$Normal central venous pressure (CVP) is generally in the range of:$x$, $x$2–8 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2–8 mmHg
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) is the pressure in the superior vena cava/right atrium, measured through a central line. It reflects RIGHT-SIDED filling pressure and is used (with other signs) to guide fluid therapy. Normal is about 2–8 mmHg (about 3–10 cmH2O). The transducer must be LEVELLED at the phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line) and zeroed, and readings are taken at end-expiration. LOW CVP suggests hypovolaemia (dehydration, bleeding). HIGH CVP suggests fluid overload, right heart failure, cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary hypertension, or high PEEP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20–30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is very HIGH — e.g. tamponade or severe right heart failure.
• 40–50 mmHg — 40–50 mmHg is impossible as a normal venous pressure.
• Below 0 mmHg — Below 0 mmHg would indicate severe hypovolaemia or a measurement error.

⭐ Key point: Normal CVP ≈ 2–8 mmHg (3–10 cmH2O). Level the transducer at the 4th ICS, mid-axillary line.$x$, null),
(66, $x$Which one of the following is NOT the cause of cardiogenic shock?$x$, $x$Spinal Cord Injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Spinal Cord Injury
CARDIOGENIC SHOCK is shock caused by the HEART failing as a PUMP — cardiac output falls despite adequate blood volume, so tissues are under-perfused. Causes are cardiac: acute MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (the commonest, usually when over 40% of the left ventricle is damaged), severe heart failure (CHF), CARDIOMYOPATHY, arrhythmias, valve rupture, myocarditis. SPINAL CORD INJURY (above T6) causes NEUROGENIC SHOCK — a type of distributive shock: loss of sympathetic tone causes vasodilation, HYPOTENSION and BRADYCARDIA with warm, dry skin. So spinal cord injury is NOT a cause of cardiogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CHF — CHF (congestive heart failure) IS a cause — a weak pump cannot keep up cardiac output.
• Cardiomyopathy — CARDIOMYOPATHY IS a cause — diseased heart muscle pumps poorly.
• MI — MI IS the most common cause of cardiogenic shock.

⭐ Key point: Cardiogenic shock = pump failure (MI, CHF, cardiomyopathy). Spinal cord injury → NEUROGENIC shock (low BP + low pulse).$x$, null),
(67, $x$CPR may be stopped in all of the following situations EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The rescuer feels the situation is hopeless$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The rescuer feels the situation is hopeless
Once CPR has started it should continue until one of these happens: (1) RETURN OF SPONTANEOUS CIRCULATION (ROSC) — the patient has a pulse; (2) a QUALIFIED person (or team, e.g. ambulance/resuscitation team) TAKES OVER; (3) a PHYSICIAN or authorised person PRONOUNCES DEATH or stops resuscitation; (4) the rescuer is physically EXHAUSTED and cannot go on, or the scene becomes UNSAFE; or (5) a valid DNACPR order is presented. Simply FEELING that the situation is hopeless is NOT a valid reason for the rescuer to stop — that is a personal emotional judgement, not a clinical decision. Survival is possible even after prolonged CPR, e.g. in hypothermia or drowning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Return of spontaneous circulation — ROSC IS a reason to stop compressions and start post-resuscitation care.
• A qualified person takes over — A qualified person TAKING OVER IS a reason to stop (they continue CPR).
• A physician pronounces death — A physician PRONOUNCING DEATH IS a reason to stop.

⭐ Key point: Stop CPR for ROSC, handover, death pronounced, exhaustion, unsafe scene or valid DNACPR — NOT because you "feel" it is hopeless.$x$, null),
(68, $x$According to 2017 ACC/AHA criteria, Stage II hypertension is defined as:$x$, $x$≥140/≥90$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ≥140/≥90
The 2017 ACC/AHA blood pressure categories are: NORMAL — below 120 and below 80; ELEVATED — 120–129 systolic and below 80; STAGE 1 hypertension — 130–139 systolic OR 80–89 diastolic; STAGE 2 hypertension — 140 or higher systolic OR 90 or higher diastolic. Hypertensive crisis — above 180 and/or above 120. Stage 2 usually needs lifestyle change PLUS medication (often two drugs). Remember to diagnose from repeated, correctly measured readings (correct cuff size, patient seated and rested 5 minutes, arm at heart level).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <120/<80 — Below 120/80 is NORMAL blood pressure.
• 120–129/<80 — 120–129/below 80 is ELEVATED blood pressure (not yet hypertension).
• 130–139/80–89 — 130–139/80–89 is STAGE 1 hypertension.

⭐ Key point: ACC/AHA 2017: Normal <120/<80; Elevated 120–129/<80; Stage 1 130–139 or 80–89; STAGE 2 ≥140 or ≥90.$x$, null),
(69, $x$While performing hemodynamic monitoring of the patient, the transducer should be kept at the Phlebostatic axis. The nurse knows that it is located at:$x$, $x$Intersection of line between 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intersection of line between 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line
The PHLEBOSTATIC AXIS is the reference point that marks the level of the RIGHT ATRIUM. It is found where a line from the 4th INTERCOSTAL SPACE at the sternal border meets the MID-AXILLARY line (half-way between the front and back of the chest) with the patient lying flat or with the head of bed up to 45°. The pressure transducer (for arterial line, CVP, pulmonary artery catheter) must be levelled and zeroed at this point. If the transducer is placed too LOW, readings are falsely HIGH; if too HIGH, readings are falsely LOW (about 0.74 mmHg per cm of error). Mark the point on the skin so all staff use the same level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intersection of line between 2nd intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 2nd intercostal space is too high — it is near the angle of Louis (sternal angle), not the right atrium level.
• Intersection of line between 3rd intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 3rd intercostal space is too high for the standard reference point.
• Intersection of line between 5th intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 5th intercostal space at the mid-axillary line is too low; using it would give falsely high readings.

⭐ Key point: Phlebostatic axis = 4th ICS + MID-AXILLARY line = level of the right atrium. Transducer low → falsely high readings.$x$, null),
(70, $x$CK-MB (creatine kinase-MB) is used clinically to help assess:$x$, $x$Recent or ongoing myocardial injury, particularly reinfarction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Recent or ongoing myocardial injury, particularly reinfarction
CK-MB is the form of creatine kinase found mainly in HEART MUSCLE. When heart muscle is damaged (myocardial infarction), CK-MB leaks into the blood: it RISES 3–6 hours after injury, PEAKS at 12–24 hours, and RETURNS TO NORMAL within 48–72 hours. Because it clears quickly, it is especially useful to detect REINFARCTION (a new heart attack a few days after the first), when troponin is still high from the first event (troponin stays raised for 7–14 days). Troponin (I or T) is now the preferred, most sensitive and specific marker for diagnosing MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kidney function — KIDNEY function is assessed with urea, creatinine and eGFR, not CK-MB.
• Coagulation status — COAGULATION status is assessed with PT/INR, aPTT, platelets and fibrinogen.
• Blood glucose control — BLOOD GLUCOSE control is assessed with glucose and HbA1c.

⭐ Key point: CK-MB = heart muscle damage; rises 3–6 h, normal by 48–72 h → useful for REINFARCTION. Troponin stays up 7–14 days.$x$, null),
(71, $x$The following are some of the drugs used in MI EXCEPT$x$, $x$Antibiotics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antibiotics
Drug treatment of MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION aims to restore blood flow, reduce heart workload, prevent new clots and protect the heart long term. Memory aid "MONA-BASH" covers the main drugs: Morphine (pain), Oxygen (only if SpO2 < 90–94%), Nitrates (GTN), Aspirin/Antiplatelets (plus clopidogrel/ticagrelor), BETA-BLOCKERS (slow the heart, reduce oxygen demand and arrhythmias), ACE inhibitors (prevent remodelling), STATINS (lower cholesterol and stabilise plaque), Heparin (anticoagulant). Thrombolytics or PCI open the blocked artery. Some CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS (verapamil, diltiazem) may be used when beta-blockers are contraindicated and to control angina. ANTIBIOTICS have no role, because MI is caused by a blocked coronary artery (thrombus on plaque), not by infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• B Blockers — BETA-BLOCKERS ARE used in MI — they reduce oxygen demand and the risk of arrhythmia and death.
• Statins — STATINS ARE used — high-intensity statin is started in all MI patients unless contraindicated.
• Ca Channel Blockers — CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS ARE used in some patients for angina/rate control when beta-blockers cannot be used.

⭐ Key point: MI drugs = MONA-BASH (morphine, oxygen, nitrates, aspirin, beta-blocker, ACE-I, statin, heparin). Antibiotics are NOT part of MI treatment.$x$, null),
(72, $x$The main aim of the Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) include all except:$x$, $x$increase myocardial O2 demand$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: increase myocardial O2 demand
The INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) is a balloon in the descending thoracic aorta. It INFLATES in DIASTOLE (right after the aortic valve closes, at the dicrotic notch), pushing blood back into the coronary arteries and forward to the body — this INCREASES CORONARY PERFUSION and oxygen supply. It DEFLATES just before SYSTOLE, creating a "vacuum" that LOWERS AFTERLOAD, so the left ventricle pumps more easily — this DECREASES MYOCARDIAL OXYGEN DEMAND and INCREASES CARDIAC OUTPUT. Better output also improves organ blood flow, including the KIDNEYS. So increasing myocardial oxygen demand is the OPPOSITE of what an IABP does. Nursing: check distal pulses of the leg, watch for limb ischaemia, keep the leg straight, check the left radial pulse (balloon too high blocks the left subclavian) and urine output (balloon too low blocks renal arteries).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• increase renal blood — Increased RENAL blood flow IS an effect, from better cardiac output.
• increasing cardiac output — INCREASING CARDIAC OUTPUT IS a main aim, through reduced afterload.
• decrease myocardial O2 demand — DECREASING myocardial oxygen demand IS a main aim — the heart works less.

⭐ Key point: IABP: inflate in diastole (↑ coronary flow), deflate before systole (↓ afterload) → ↓ O2 DEMAND, ↑ cardiac output.$x$, null),
(73, $x$Trending serial lactate levels during shock resuscitation is useful because:$x$, $x$Lactate clearance correlates with improving tissue perfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lactate clearance correlates with improving tissue perfusion
LACTATE is made when tissues don't get enough oxygen and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism. In shock, a raised lactate (above 2 mmol/L; above 4 is severe) shows tissue HYPOPERFUSION. Measuring it SERIALLY (e.g. every 2–4 hours) shows whether resuscitation is working: a FALLING lactate (LACTATE CLEARANCE, e.g. a drop of 10–20% or more) means perfusion is improving and is linked to better survival; a static or rising lactate means the patient still needs more treatment (fluids, vasopressors, source control). This is why the Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends re-measuring lactate if the first value is raised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lactate has no relationship to perfusion status — Lactate DOES relate to perfusion — that is exactly why it is measured in shock.
• It replaces the need for vital sign monitoring — It does NOT replace vital signs monitoring; it is used together with BP, heart rate, urine output, capillary refill and mental state.
• It only reflects liver function — The liver clears lactate, so liver failure can raise it, but it mainly reflects tissue perfusion/oxygen delivery, not "only" liver function.

⭐ Key point: Falling lactate (lactate clearance) = improving tissue perfusion. Rising lactate = ongoing hypoperfusion.$x$, null),
(74, $x$Which test is performed in order to check collateral circulation, before going to pass arterial line catheter in radial artery;$x$, $x$Allen test$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Allen test
The ALLEN TEST checks that the hand has a good blood supply from the ULNAR artery before the RADIAL artery is punctured or cannulated (arterial line or ABG). Steps: (1) ask the patient to make a tight fist; (2) press firmly on both the radial and ulnar arteries at the wrist; (3) the patient opens the hand — the palm looks pale; (4) RELEASE the ULNAR artery only; (5) colour should return to the palm within about 5–15 seconds (positive/normal test = good collateral flow). If colour takes longer than about 15 seconds, collateral circulation is poor and the radial artery should NOT be used, because if it clots, the hand could become ischaemic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Romberg test — The ROMBERG test checks balance/proprioception (patient stands with feet together and eyes closed).
• Weber test — The WEBER test uses a tuning fork on the forehead to check for conductive or sensorineural hearing loss.
• Whisper test — The WHISPER test is a simple screening test for hearing.

⭐ Key point: Before a radial arterial line or ABG → ALLEN TEST (checks ulnar collateral flow; colour back within 5–15 s).$x$, null),
(75, $x$Which of the following descriptions correctly characterizes the cardiac glycosides (e.g. digoxin)?$x$, $x$POSITIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: POSITIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE
DIGOXIN (a cardiac glycoside) has two main heart effects. (1) POSITIVE INOTROPE: it blocks the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump in heart muscle; this raises calcium inside the cells, so the heart contracts more STRONGLY — helpful in heart failure. (2) NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE: it increases vagal (parasympathetic) tone and slows conduction through the AV node, so the HEART RATE FALLS — helpful to control the rate in atrial fibrillation. Nursing: check the APICAL pulse for a full minute before giving and hold if below 60 bpm (adults); monitor potassium (LOW K⁺ increases digoxin toxicity) and digoxin levels (0.5–2 ng/mL). Toxicity signs: nausea, vomiting, anorexia, yellow-green vision (xanthopsia), confusion, bradycardia and arrhythmias; antidote is digoxin-specific antibody (DigiFab).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• POSITIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Positive inotrope, positive chronotrope" describes drugs like dobutamine/adrenaline — digoxin SLOWS the heart.
• NEGATIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Negative inotrope, negative chronotrope" describes beta-blockers or verapamil — digoxin STRENGTHENS contraction.
• NEGATIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Negative inotrope, positive chronotrope" is the opposite of digoxin on both counts.

⭐ Key point: Digoxin = POSITIVE inotrope (stronger) + NEGATIVE chronotrope (slower). Check apical pulse; hold if < 60; low K⁺ → toxicity.$x$, null),
(76, $x$In critical care, what is the primary purpose of a pulmonary artery catheter (PAC)?$x$, $x$D) Assessing cardiac function and pressures$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: D) Assessing cardiac function and pressures
A PULMONARY ARTERY CATHETER (PAC, Swan-Ganz) is a long, balloon-tipped catheter passed through a central vein, the right atrium and right ventricle into the pulmonary artery. It is used to ASSESS CARDIAC FUNCTION AND PRESSURES: right atrial pressure (CVP), pulmonary artery pressure, pulmonary artery WEDGE pressure (PAWP/PCWP — an estimate of left atrial/left ventricular filling pressure, normal 6–12 mmHg), CARDIAC OUTPUT (by thermodilution), systemic and pulmonary vascular resistance, and mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2). It helps guide fluids, inotropes and vasopressors in complex shock, cardiac surgery and severe heart failure. Risks: arrhythmias, infection, pulmonary artery rupture, pulmonary infarction (never leave the balloon wedged).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring brain activity — Monitoring BRAIN activity is done with EEG, not a PAC.
• B) Administering anesthesia — ANAESTHESIA is given by IV drugs, inhaled gases or nerve blocks, not by a PAC.
• C) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP) — INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE is measured with an ICP monitor (intraventricular drain or parenchymal probe), not a PAC.

⭐ Key point: PAC (Swan-Ganz) = assesses CARDIAC FUNCTION: PA pressure, wedge pressure, cardiac output, SvO2.$x$, null),
(77, $x$The juxtaglomerular apparatus regulates blood pressure and renal blood flow chiefly by secreting which substance?$x$, $x$Renin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Renin
The JUXTAGLOMERULAR APPARATUS (JGA) sits where the afferent arteriole touches the distal tubule of each nephron. Its JUXTAGLOMERULAR cells release RENIN when they sense LOW blood pressure/low renal blood flow, low sodium (detected by the macula densa) or sympathetic stimulation. Renin starts the RENIN–ANGIOTENSIN–ALDOSTERONE SYSTEM (RAAS): renin converts angiotensinogen (from the liver) to angiotensin I; ACE (mainly in the lungs) converts it to ANGIOTENSIN II, which constricts blood vessels and triggers ALDOSTERONE release (sodium and water retention) and ADH and thirst — together raising BP and restoring kidney perfusion. ACE inhibitors and ARBs block this system to treat hypertension and heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antidiuretic hormone — ADH (antidiuretic hormone) is made in the hypothalamus and released from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY, not the JGA.
• Aldosterone — ALDOSTERONE is made in the ADRENAL CORTEX (zona glomerulosa); the JGA starts the chain that leads to it but does not secrete it.
• Erythropoietin — ERYTHROPOIETIN is made by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney in response to low oxygen; it stimulates red cell production, not BP control.

⭐ Key point: JGA secretes RENIN → angiotensin II → aldosterone → ↑ BP. ACE-I/ARBs block this pathway.$x$, null),
(78, $x$Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT), compared to intermittent haemodialysis, is often preferred in haemodynamically unstable ICU patients because it:$x$, $x$Removes fluid/solutes more slowly and continuously, causing less haemodynamic stress$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Removes fluid/solutes more slowly and continuously, causing less haemodynamic stress
CRRT (Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy, e.g. CVVH, CVVHD, CVVHDF) runs 24 hours a day and removes fluid and waste SLOWLY and CONTINUOUSLY. Because fluid is removed gently over many hours (e.g. 50–200 mL/hour), there are smaller shifts of fluid and solutes, so BP stays more STABLE. This makes it the preferred method in HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE ICU patients (shock, on vasopressors), and in patients with brain injury (less risk of raised ICP from rapid osmotic shifts). Intermittent haemodialysis (IHD) removes the same amount in 3–4 hours, which can cause sudden HYPOTENSION in unstable patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is always faster at removing toxins — CRRT is NOT faster; IHD is faster at clearing toxins, which is why IHD is preferred for some poisonings and severe hyperkalaemia.
• Requires no vascular access — CRRT DOES need vascular access — a large double-lumen dialysis catheter (femoral, internal jugular).
• Has no risk of complications — CRRT has risks: bleeding from anticoagulation, filter clotting, hypothermia, electrolyte imbalance (low K⁺, low phosphate), line infection.

⭐ Key point: CRRT = slow, continuous removal → less haemodynamic stress → best for unstable ICU patients. IHD is faster but causes more hypotension.$x$, null),
(79, $x$At 10 years, survival with a living-related renal transplant is approximately:$x$, $x$74.84%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 74.84%
Kidney transplant survival depends on the donor type. A LIVING-RELATED (living) donor kidney gives better results than a deceased-donor kidney because the kidney is healthy, carefully matched, and transplanted with very short cold ischaemia time. Long-term data show about 75% (here 74.84%) of patients/grafts surviving at 10 YEARS after living-related transplant, compared with lower figures for deceased donors (around 55–60%). Survival also depends on matching (HLA), immunosuppression adherence, prevention of rejection and infection, and control of BP and diabetes. Nurses play a big role in teaching patients to take immunosuppressants on time and report signs of rejection (fever, reduced urine, graft pain, rising creatinine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8.49% — 8.49% is far too low; this would mean almost every transplant fails within 10 years, which is not true.
• 55.07% — 55.07% is closer to deceased-donor 10-year survival, not living-related.
• 100% — 100% is impossible; some grafts are always lost through rejection, infection or death from other causes.

⭐ Key point: Living-related kidney transplant: about 75% survival at 10 years — better than deceased donor (~55–60%).$x$, null),
(80, $x$The following are causes of Normal sized or enlarged Kidneys in CKD Except$x$, $x$Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis
In most CKD the kidneys SHRINK (become small and scarred) as nephrons are lost. However, some causes keep the kidneys NORMAL-SIZED or ENLARGED even in advanced disease — memory aid: "DIABETIC PAH": Diabetic nephropathy, HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN), AMYLOIDOSIS (protein deposits), ADPKD (adult polycystic kidney disease — big cyst-filled kidneys), plus myeloma kidney, and sickle-cell nephropathy. HYPERTENSIVE NEPHROSCLEROSIS causes SMALL, shrunken kidneys, because long-standing high BP damages small arteries and causes scarring of the glomeruli. On ultrasound, small kidneys (under about 9 cm) usually mean chronic, irreversible disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HIVAN — HIVAN typically causes LARGE, echogenic kidneys — it does belong to the normal/enlarged group.
• ADPKD — ADPKD causes very LARGE kidneys full of cysts.
• Amyloidosis — AMYLOIDOSIS causes normal or LARGE kidneys due to amyloid deposits.

⭐ Key point: CKD with normal/large kidneys = diabetes, HIVAN, amyloid, ADPKD, myeloma. HYPERTENSIVE nephrosclerosis = SMALL kidneys.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Post-nephrectomy donor investigations typically include:$x$, $x$Chest X-ray, FBC, Electrolytes/Urea/Creatinine, eGFR$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chest X-ray, FBC, Electrolytes/Urea/Creatinine, eGFR
A live kidney donor must be fully assessed so that the operation is safe and the remaining single kidney will work well. Standard investigations (pre- and peri-operative) include: CHEST X-RAY (heart and lungs before anaesthesia), FULL BLOOD COUNT (anaemia, infection), ELECTROLYTES, UREA and CREATININE, and eGFR (kidney function — to make sure the remaining kidney is adequate), plus blood group and cross-match, urinalysis, viral screening (HIV, hepatitis B/C), ECG, and CT angiography of the kidneys before surgery. After nephrectomy, creatinine and eGFR are monitored closely because the remaining kidney must take over the full workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a urine dipstick — A urine dipstick ALONE is far too little to assess kidney function and fitness for surgery.
• No pre-operative investigations are needed — Saying "no investigations are needed" is unsafe; major surgery always needs assessment.
• Bone marrow biopsy — BONE MARROW BIOPSY is for blood disorders (e.g. leukaemia), not kidney donation.

⭐ Key point: Kidney donor work-up = CXR, FBC, U&E/creatinine, eGFR, blood group, viral screen, ECG — check the remaining kidney is adequate.$x$, null),
(82, $x$Which of the following is the most common intrinsic (intrarenal) cause of acute kidney injury in critically ill patients?$x$, $x$Acute tubular necrosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute tubular necrosis
AKI causes are grouped as: PRE-RENAL (low blood flow to kidneys — dehydration, haemorrhage, shock, heart failure), INTRINSIC/intrarenal (damage inside the kidney), and POST-RENAL (blockage of urine flow). In critically ill patients, the most common INTRINSIC cause is ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (ATN). ATN happens when kidney tubule cells die from prolonged ISCHAEMIA (e.g. untreated pre-renal shock/sepsis) or TOXINS (aminoglycosides, IV contrast, rhabdomyolysis/myoglobin, NSAIDs). Signs: oliguria, rising creatinine, muddy brown granular casts in urine, urine sodium over 40 mmol/L. Management: treat the cause, optimise fluids and BP, avoid nephrotoxins, adjust drug doses, dialysis if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bladder outlet obstruction — BLADDER OUTLET OBSTRUCTION (e.g. enlarged prostate) is a POST-RENAL cause.
• Renal artery stenosis — RENAL ARTERY STENOSIS reduces blood flow to the kidney — usually considered PRE-RENAL/vascular, and usually chronic.
• Nephrolithiasis — NEPHROLITHIASIS (kidney stones) causes POST-RENAL obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Commonest intrinsic AKI in ICU = ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (from ischaemia/sepsis or toxins). Muddy brown casts.$x$, null),
(83, $x$What is the first step in the pharmacologic management approach to ICU delirium?$x$, $x$Identify and treat underlying reversible causes (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic derangement) before adding antipsychotics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Identify and treat underlying reversible causes (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic derangement) before adding antipsychotics
DELIRIUM is an acute change in attention and thinking, with a fluctuating course. It is almost always caused by something — so the FIRST step is to look for and TREAT REVERSIBLE CAUSES. Memory aid "I WATCH DEATH" or "PINCH ME": Pain, Infection (sepsis, UTI, pneumonia), Nutrition/dehydration, Constipation, HYPOXIA, Medications (benzodiazepines, anticholinergics, opioids), Environment, Electrolyte/metabolic disturbances (sodium, glucose, calcium), urinary retention, withdrawal (alcohol). Also use non-drug measures: reorientation, clocks and calendars, glasses and hearing aids, family visits, sleep at night/light in day, early mobilisation (ABCDEF bundle). ANTIPSYCHOTICS (e.g. haloperidol, quetiapine) are only for severe agitation that puts the patient or staff at risk, at the lowest dose for the shortest time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately start high-dose benzodiazepines — High-dose BENZODIAZEPINES can CAUSE or WORSEN delirium (except in alcohol/benzodiazepine withdrawal).
• Start antipsychotics for all patients regardless of cause — Antipsychotics for ALL patients regardless of cause is wrong; they don't fix the cause and have side effects (QT prolongation, sedation).
• Sedate to RASS -5 to prevent movement — Deep sedation to RASS −5 increases delirium, ventilator days and mortality; the target is light sedation (RASS 0 to −2).

⭐ Key point: ICU delirium → FIRST find and treat the cause (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic, drugs). Antipsychotics only for severe agitation.$x$, null),
(84, $x$Status epilepticus is defined as a seizure lasting longer than:$x$, $x$5 minutes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5 minutes
STATUS EPILEPTICUS (SE) is now defined as a seizure lasting MORE THAN 5 MINUTES, or two or more seizures without full recovery of consciousness in between. Older textbooks said 30 minutes, but the definition was shortened because most seizures stop within 2–3 minutes; after 5 minutes they are unlikely to stop on their own, and after about 30 minutes there is risk of permanent brain damage. Treatment timeline: 0–5 min — ABC, oxygen, check glucose, IV access; 5–20 min — BENZODIAZEPINE (IV lorazepam 4 mg, or buccal/IV midazolam, or diazepam), may repeat once; 20–40 min — second-line drug (levetiracetam, phenytoin/fosphenytoin, valproate); over 40 min (refractory) — anaesthetic agents and intubation in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 seconds — 30 seconds is an ordinary short seizure.
• 2 minutes — 2 minutes is still within the usual length of a self-limiting seizure.
• 15 minutes — 15 minutes is longer than the current definition; waiting this long delays treatment.

⭐ Key point: Status epilepticus = seizure > 5 MINUTES (or repeated without recovery). First-line: IV lorazepam/midazolam.$x$, null),
(85, $x$A patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale eye-opening score of 2 demonstrates:$x$, $x$Eye opening to pain only$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Eye opening to pain only
The GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) has three parts, total 3–15. EYE OPENING (E, 1–4): 4 = spontaneous; 3 = to voice/speech; 2 = to PAIN (pressure) only; 1 = none. VERBAL (V, 1–5): 5 oriented, 4 confused, 3 inappropriate words, 2 incomprehensible sounds, 1 none. MOTOR (M, 1–6): 6 obeys commands, 5 localises pain, 4 withdraws (normal flexion), 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none. So E2 means EYES OPEN ONLY TO A PAINFUL STIMULUS (e.g. trapezius squeeze, supraorbital pressure, nail-bed pressure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spontaneous eye opening — SPONTANEOUS eye opening = E4.
• Eye opening to voice — Eye opening to VOICE = E3.
• No eye opening at all — NO eye opening even to pain = E1.

⭐ Key point: GCS eyes: 4 spontaneous, 3 voice, 2 PAIN, 1 none. Verbal max 5; motor max 6; total 3–15.$x$, null),
(86, $x$The coup-contrecoup mechanism of brain injury describes which of the following?$x$, $x$Injury at the site of impact and at the opposite pole of the brain from movement within the skull$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Injury at the site of impact and at the opposite pole of the brain from movement within the skull
In a COUP-CONTRECOUP injury, the head is hit (or suddenly stops, e.g. in a car crash). The brain hits the inside of the skull at the point of impact (COUP injury), then rebounds and hits the OPPOSITE side of the skull (CONTRECOUP injury). So bruises (contusions) appear at BOTH the site of impact AND the opposite pole of the brain. Example: a fall onto the back of the head often causes contusions in the FRONTAL and TEMPORAL lobes at the front. Nursing: assess the whole brain function, not just the impact area; watch for delayed swelling and rising ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Injury only at the site of direct impact — Injury ONLY at the site of direct impact is a coup injury alone — it ignores the contrecoup part.
• Diffuse shearing injury without focal contusion — DIFFUSE SHEARING without focal contusion describes DIFFUSE AXONAL INJURY (from rotational acceleration), not coup-contrecoup.
• A fracture line radiating from the impact point — A fracture line radiating from the impact point describes a LINEAR SKULL FRACTURE, not brain contusion.

⭐ Key point: Coup-contrecoup = injury at the IMPACT site AND the OPPOSITE side of the brain from rebound inside the skull.$x$, null),
(87, $x$Autonomic dysreflexia in a patient with a spinal cord injury above T6 is typically triggered by:$x$, $x$A noxious stimulus below the level of injury, such as a distended bladder$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A noxious stimulus below the level of injury, such as a distended bladder
AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA (AD) happens in spinal cord injury at or ABOVE T6. A NOXIOUS (irritating) stimulus BELOW the injury level — most commonly a FULL BLADDER (blocked or kinked catheter), then a full bowel/constipation, pressure ulcer, tight clothes, ingrown toenail, sunburn — causes a massive sympathetic reflex below the injury. Blood vessels constrict, causing SEVERE HYPERTENSION (can be over 200 mmHg — risk of stroke, seizure, death). The brain tries to respond through the vagus (BRADYCARDIA) and vasodilation above the lesion (POUNDING HEADACHE, FLUSHING and SWEATING above the injury, nasal congestion), but the message cannot pass down the damaged cord. Action: SIT THE PATIENT UP (lower the legs), loosen tight clothing, find and remove the cause (check catheter, empty bladder, check bowel), monitor BP every 2–5 minutes, give antihypertensive (e.g. nifedipine, GTN) if BP stays high.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild anxiety only — MILD ANXIETY alone does not trigger this dangerous reflex.
• Normal ambient room temperature — Normal room temperature is not a noxious stimulus.
• Eating a regular meal — Eating a normal meal is not a trigger (although a full bowel/constipation can be).

⭐ Key point: Autonomic dysreflexia (SCI ≥ T6) = noxious stimulus below injury, usually FULL BLADDER → severe hypertension. SIT UP, find the cause.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Hypothalamic damage may cause all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Facial paresis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Facial paresis
The HYPOTHALAMUS controls the AUTONOMIC nervous system and hormones. Damage (trauma, tumour, stroke, surgery) can cause: temperature problems (hyperthermia/hypothermia), SWEATING disorders, BLOOD PRESSURE swings, CARDIAC RHYTHM disturbances (tachycardia, arrhythmias — part of paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity after brain injury), diabetes insipidus or SIADH, appetite and sleep problems. FACIAL PARESIS (weakness of facial muscles) comes from damage to the FACIAL NERVE (cranial nerve VII) itself — e.g. Bell's palsy — or to the motor cortex/corticobulbar tract (stroke); it is not caused by hypothalamic damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sweating disorders — SWEATING disorders CAN occur — the hypothalamus controls sweating for temperature regulation.
• Blood pressure disturbance — BLOOD PRESSURE disturbances CAN occur — the hypothalamus controls autonomic tone.
• Cardiac rhythm disturbance — CARDIAC RHYTHM disturbances CAN occur through autonomic dysregulation.

⭐ Key point: Hypothalamic damage → temperature, sweating, BP, heart rhythm, water balance problems. Facial paresis = facial nerve/cortex, not hypothalamus.$x$, null),
(89, $x$Bilaterally pinpoint pupils in an unresponsive patient may suggest:$x$, $x$Opioid toxicity or a pontine lesion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Opioid toxicity or a pontine lesion
PINPOINT PUPILS (very small, under 2 mm) on both sides in an unconscious patient suggest two main causes: (1) OPIOID TOXICITY (morphine, heroin, fentanyl, tramadol) — the classic triad is pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and reduced consciousness; give NALOXONE (0.4 mg IV, repeat as needed). Organophosphate poisoning also causes small pupils. (2) A PONTINE LESION (e.g. pontine haemorrhage or infarction) — this damages descending sympathetic fibres, so the pupils constrict; the patient is often deeply comatose with high temperature. Always check blood glucose and consider naloxone trial.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic drug effect — ANTICHOLINERGIC drugs (atropine, tricyclic antidepressants, antihistamines) cause DILATED pupils ("blind as a bat, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare, dry as a bone").
• Normal resting pupil size in bright light only — Normal pupils in bright light are small but not pinpoint, and an UNRESPONSIVE patient with pinpoint pupils is not "normal".
• Complete brain death in all cases — BRAIN DEATH usually shows FIXED, mid-sized or DILATED pupils, not pinpoint.

⭐ Key point: Bilateral PINPOINT pupils + coma = OPIOIDS (give naloxone) or PONTINE lesion. Dilated pupils = anticholinergic, brain death, herniation.$x$, null),
(90, $x$Which of the following is a preventable secondary brain injury factor that critical care nurses must aggressively manage after TBI?$x$, $x$Hypotension and hypoxia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension and hypoxia
In TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY (TBI), PRIMARY injury happens at the moment of impact and cannot be undone. SECONDARY injury develops over hours to days and can be PREVENTED — this is the main focus of critical care. The most damaging secondary factors are HYPOTENSION (systolic below 90–110 mmHg) and HYPOXIA (SpO2 below 90% or PaO2 below 60 mmHg): even one episode greatly increases death and disability, because the injured brain cannot tolerate low oxygen delivery. Other secondary factors: high or low CO2, fever, seizures, hypo/hyperglycaemia, low sodium, raised ICP, anaemia. Nursing aims: SpO2 ≥ 94%, systolic BP ≥ 100–110 mmHg, CPP 60–70 mmHg, normocapnia (PaCO2 35–45), normothermia, glucose control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The initial mechanical impact — The INITIAL MECHANICAL IMPACT is the PRIMARY injury — it has already happened and cannot be prevented by nurses.
• Skull fracture location — SKULL FRACTURE LOCATION is fixed at the time of injury; it guides assessment but is not modifiable.
• Scalp laceration — A SCALP LACERATION needs care (it can bleed heavily) but is not a secondary brain injury factor.

⭐ Key point: Prevent SECONDARY brain injury: avoid HYPOTENSION and HYPOXIA (also high/low CO2, fever, seizures, glucose problems).$x$, null),
(91, $x$The pupillary light reflex tests cranial nerves:$x$, $x$II and III$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: II and III
The PUPILLARY LIGHT REFLEX has two nerves: the AFFERENT (sensory) limb is the OPTIC NERVE (CN II), which carries the light signal from the retina to the brainstem; the EFFERENT (motor) limb is the OCULOMOTOR NERVE (CN III), whose parasympathetic fibres make the pupil CONSTRICT. Shining light in one eye normally makes BOTH pupils constrict (direct and consensual response). A FIXED, DILATED pupil on one side in a head-injured patient often means CN III is being compressed by uncal HERNIATION — a neurosurgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• I and II — CN I (olfactory) is smell and CN II is vision; CN I is not part of the light reflex.
• IV and V — CN IV (trochlear) moves the eye down and in; CN V (trigeminal) is facial sensation and chewing — the corneal reflex uses V, not the light reflex.
• IV and VI — CN IV and CN VI (abducens) control eye movements, not pupil size.

⭐ Key point: Pupillary light reflex: CN II in (sensory), CN III out (motor). Unilateral fixed dilated pupil = CN III compression (herniation).$x$, null),
(92, $x$Your patient's eyes do not open to noxious stimuli and they respond incorrectly when asked questions and localize to pain. What is their GCS?$x$, $x$9$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 9
Score each part of the GCS: EYES — do not open even to noxious (painful) stimulus = E1. VERBAL — "responds INCORRECTLY when asked questions" is scored by this exam key as INAPPROPRIATE WORDS = V3 (words that do not answer the question). MOTOR — LOCALISES to pain (moves a hand towards the painful stimulus to remove it) = M5. Total: E1 + V3 + M5 = 9. Exam tip: if the stem had said "confused but answers in sentences", that would be V4 (total 10) — so read the verbal description carefully. Remember GCS ≤ 8 = coma/"intubate", 9–12 moderate, 13–15 mild brain injury. Always document each component (E1 V3 M5), not just the total.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7 — 7 would need lower scores (e.g. E1 V2 M4) — it underestimates this patient.
• 8 — 8 would be E1 V2 M5 or E1 V3 M4 — it does not match "localises to pain" with words.
• 10 — 10 would only be correct if the verbal response were scored as "confused" (V4); the key here takes it as inappropriate words (V3).

⭐ Key point: GCS = E(1–4) + V(1–5) + M(1–6). Here E1 + V3 + M5 = 9. Always report each part.$x$, null),
(93, $x$What degree of head-of-bed elevation is recommended to reduce intracranial pressure while maintaining cerebral perfusion?$x$, $x$30 degrees of elevation with neutral head and neck alignment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 30 degrees of elevation with neutral head and neck alignment
To lower INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP) while keeping CEREBRAL PERFUSION, raise the HEAD OF BED to about 30 DEGREES and keep the HEAD and NECK in a NEUTRAL (midline) position. This helps venous blood drain from the brain through the jugular veins, lowering ICP, without dropping BP too much (CPP = MAP − ICP; target CPP 60–70 mmHg). Avoid neck flexion or rotation, tight ETT ties or cervical collars that compress the jugular veins. Other measures: avoid hip flexion over 90°, keep the patient calm and pain-free, cluster nursing care, avoid coughing/straining, normothermia, normal CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flat at 0 degrees — FLAT at 0° reduces venous drainage and raises ICP.
• 15 degrees of elevation — 15° is less effective for venous drainage than 30°.
• 90 degrees, fully upright — 90° fully upright can drop MAP and cerebral perfusion pressure, and is uncomfortable/unsafe in unstable patients.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP → head of bed 30° + NEUTRAL head/neck alignment (no jugular compression).$x$, null),
(94, $x$The nurse is preparing to suction the airway of an unconscious patient. The nurse should be aware that excessive suctioning can lead to:$x$, $x$Hypoxemia and cardiac arrhythmias$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxemia and cardiac arrhythmias
SUCTIONING removes secretions, but it also removes OXYGEN from the airway and lungs, and the catheter stimulates the vagus nerve. Excessive or prolonged suctioning can therefore cause HYPOXAEMIA (low blood oxygen) and CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIAS (bradycardia from vagal stimulation, or tachyarrhythmias from hypoxia). Other risks: atelectasis (lung collapse), mucosal trauma and bleeding, infection, bronchospasm and raised ICP. Safe practice: suction ONLY when needed (secretions heard/seen, falling SpO2, high airway pressures), PRE-OXYGENATE with 100% O2 for 30–60 seconds, use the right catheter size (no more than half the ETT diameter), apply suction for no more than 10–15 SECONDS, use pressure 80–150 mmHg (adults), monitor SpO2 and ECG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension and tachycardia — HYPERTENSION and TACHYCARDIA can occur briefly but are not the classic main danger.
• Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis — Suctioning does NOT cause hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis; it takes away ventilation.
• Increased intracranial pressure — Suctioning can raise ICP temporarily, but the most important general risks are hypoxaemia and arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: Excessive suctioning → HYPOXAEMIA + ARRHYTHMIAS (bradycardia). Pre-oxygenate; max 10–15 s per pass.$x$, null),
(95, $x$Which of the following findings is NOT compatible with a diagnosis of brain death?$x$, $x$Spontaneous respiratory effort during apnea testing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous respiratory effort during apnea testing
BRAIN DEATH (brainstem death) means permanent loss of all brain and brainstem function. Criteria include: known cause and exclusion of reversible factors (sedatives, hypothermia, metabolic problems); deep coma with no response; ABSENT brainstem reflexes — FIXED pupils (unreactive to light), ABSENT CORNEAL reflex, absent oculocephalic/oculovestibular reflexes, ABSENT GAG and cough reflexes, no motor response in the cranial nerve area; and a positive APNOEA TEST — no breathing effort when PaCO2 is allowed to rise above 60 mmHg (or 6.65 kPa per UK criteria) and pH is below 7.30. Any SPONTANEOUS BREATHING EFFORT during apnoea testing means the brainstem respiratory centre is still working, so brain death CANNOT be diagnosed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fixed and dilated pupils — FIXED DILATED pupils ARE consistent with brain death.
• Absent corneal reflex — ABSENT CORNEAL reflex IS consistent with brain death.
• Absent gag reflex — ABSENT GAG reflex IS consistent with brain death.

⭐ Key point: Any SPONTANEOUS BREATHING during the apnoea test rules out brain death.$x$, null),
(96, $x$When does primary brain injury occur?$x$, $x$At the moment of impact$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: At the moment of impact
PRIMARY BRAIN INJURY is the damage caused at the MOMENT OF IMPACT — the direct mechanical force on the brain tissue: contusions, lacerations, skull fractures, diffuse axonal injury, tearing of blood vessels. It happens in milliseconds and cannot be reversed by treatment; only prevention (helmets, seatbelts, road safety) helps. SECONDARY BRAIN INJURY develops minutes, hours or days later from hypoxia, hypotension, swelling, raised ICP, seizures, fever, infection and metabolic problems — this is what medical and nursing care tries to prevent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In the first hour after injury — "In the first hour" describes the time when SECONDARY injury may begin, not primary injury.
• In the first 4 hours after injury — "In the first 4 hours" is also the secondary-injury window, not the primary event.
• Only if the patient is in coma — Primary injury happens in every brain injury at impact, whether or not the patient is in coma.

⭐ Key point: PRIMARY brain injury = at the MOMENT OF IMPACT (cannot be undone). SECONDARY = later, preventable.$x$, null),
(97, $x$Which type of ICU delirium is most commonly MISSED by staff?$x$, $x$Hypoactive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypoactive
Delirium has three types. HYPERACTIVE — agitated, restless, pulling at lines, aggressive; easy to notice. HYPOACTIVE — quiet, withdrawn, sleepy, slow, staring, little movement or speech; the patient seems "calm" or "just tired" or depressed, so it is often MISSED by staff, even though it is the MOST COMMON type in ICU and older patients and has the WORST outcome (higher mortality, longer stay). MIXED — swings between the two. That is why every ICU patient should be screened regularly (e.g. every shift) with a tool like the CAM-ICU or ICDSC, not just those who are agitated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperactive — HYPERACTIVE delirium is the most noticeable type because of agitation, so it is rarely missed.
• Mixed — MIXED delirium is usually noticed during the hyperactive phases.
• All types are equally easy to spot — Not all types are equally easy to spot — hypoactive is clearly the hardest.

⭐ Key point: HYPOACTIVE delirium (quiet, sleepy) is the most commonly MISSED. Screen all patients with CAM-ICU.$x$, null),
(98, $x$A patient with hyperactive delirium is prescribed quetiapine. The nurse monitors for:$x$, $x$A and C$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and C
QUETIAPINE is an atypical (second-generation) ANTIPSYCHOTIC sometimes used for agitated ICU delirium. Key monitoring: (1) QT PROLONGATION — antipsychotics can lengthen the QT interval, risking torsades de pointes (a dangerous ventricular arrhythmia); check a baseline ECG and repeat it, keep potassium and magnesium normal, avoid other QT-prolonging drugs (e.g. haloperidol, macrolides, ondansetron). (2) HYPERGLYCAEMIA and metabolic effects — atypical antipsychotics can raise blood sugar and lipids; monitor glucose. Other side effects: sedation, ORTHOSTATIC HYPOTENSION (low BP, not high), anticholinergic effects, and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• QT prolongation — QT prolongation alone is correct but incomplete, since hyperglycaemia must also be monitored.
• Hypertension — HYPERTENSION is not a typical effect; quetiapine more often causes HYPOTENSION (postural drop) from alpha-blockade.
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia alone is correct but incomplete, since QT prolongation is also a key risk.

⭐ Key point: Quetiapine/antipsychotics: monitor QT (ECG, K⁺, Mg²⁺) AND blood GLUCOSE. They cause low BP, not high.$x$, null),
(99, $x$Emergence delirium following general anaesthesia is characterised by:$x$, $x$Agitation, confusion, and thrashing shortly after waking$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Agitation, confusion, and thrashing shortly after waking
EMERGENCE DELIRIUM (emergence agitation) happens in the first minutes as a patient WAKES from general anaesthesia: they are AGITATED, CONFUSED, crying, restless, THRASHING about, may pull at drains, lines and the airway, and do not recognise surroundings. It usually settles within 10–30 minutes. It is more common in children, young adults, after volatile agents (sevoflurane), with pain, full bladder, hypoxia, and in patients with PTSD or anxiety. Nursing: protect the patient from injury and falls (side rails, stay close), check and treat causes (pain, HYPOXIA, hypercapnia, hypoglycaemia, full bladder), calm reassurance, and small doses of sedatives/opioids as prescribed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calm, oriented behaviour — Calm, oriented behaviour is NORMAL recovery, not emergence delirium.
• Prolonged unconsciousness — Prolonged unconsciousness is DELAYED EMERGENCE, a different problem.
• Normal cognitive function throughout — Normal cognitive function throughout means no delirium is present.

⭐ Key point: Emergence delirium = agitation, confusion and thrashing right after waking from anaesthesia. Protect from injury; check for hypoxia, pain, full bladder.$x$, null),
(100, $x$A patient recovering from surgery develops confusion, agitation, and disorientation on post-operative day 2, with no prior history of cognitive impairment. This is most consistent with:$x$, $x$Postoperative delirium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Postoperative delirium
POSTOPERATIVE DELIRIUM is an ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention, awareness and thinking that starts after surgery — typically on POST-OP DAYS 1–3 — in a patient who was previously cognitively normal. Signs: confusion, disorientation, agitation (or quietness), poor attention, sleep–wake disturbance, sometimes hallucinations. Risk factors: older age, major surgery, pain, opioids/benzodiazepines, infection, dehydration, hypoxia, electrolyte imbalance, urinary retention, sleep deprivation, alcohol withdrawal. It is usually reversible when the cause is treated, but it increases complications and length of stay. Screen with CAM, then look for and treat the cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Baseline dementia — BASELINE DEMENTIA is a long-standing, slowly progressive problem; this patient had NO previous cognitive impairment.
• Normal anaesthesia recovery expected for weeks — Normal anaesthesia recovery takes minutes to hours, not confusion lasting for weeks; day-2 confusion is NOT normal.
• A permanent neurological condition — A permanent neurological condition is unlikely; delirium is acute and usually reversible (though a stroke must be excluded if there are focal signs).

⭐ Key point: New confusion/agitation on post-op day 1–3 in a previously normal patient = POSTOPERATIVE DELIRIUM. Find and treat the cause.$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
select count(distinct n) as "questions updated (should be 100)", count(*) as "rows changed" from upd;
